(window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS=window.LING406_MODERN_PACKS||{})["m3-n93"]={"id":"n93","label":"Notes 9.3 · clearest slide-by-slide notes","revision":"ling406-modern-m3-n93-2","scripts":{"m3-reading1-p001":[],"m3-reading1-p002":[{"text":"**Phonemes** are speech-sound categories, not letters. A writing system represents a language, but its symbols do not have to match those categories one to one.","children":["A single letter can take part in different pronunciations.","A single phoneme can be written with different spellings."]},{"text":"So the first thing to remember about phonemes is that **phonemes are not letters**.","children":["Identifying a letter is a different task from identifying a phoneme.","The question is about how sound relates to writing. It is not about how many letters a word has."]},{"text":"That is why spoken categories and written symbols are kept separate.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p003":[{"text":"A **mispronouncing contest** tested whether letters and phonemes line up. In it, people willfully mispronounced words such as COLONEL and PARADIGM.","children":["Reading COLONEL by its usual letter patterns gives a wrong pronunciation.","Reading PARADIGM the same way also gives a wrong pronunciation.","So phonemes are not letters, and **letters are not phonemes** either."]},{"text":"**Orthographic depth** is how consistently a script's spelling matches its pronunciation.","children":["For **English**, many spellings do not clearly specify their sounds. So English is a relatively **deep** script, because of its irregularities.","For **Finnish** and **Korean**, spelling matches sound very predictably. So they are very **shallow** scripts.","**Pinyin** for Mandarin and **French** are also relatively regular."]},{"text":"A deep script is less predictable, but it is not random. The difference concerns the writing system, not the ability of its readers. That is why an unfamiliar English spelling cannot always be decoded from its letters alone.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p004":[{"text":"**Finnish** has a very **transparent** writing system. The **Orthographic Depth Hypothesis** calls this a **shallow** system.","children":["Each letter matches one phoneme more or less unambiguously.","Learning one letter-sound match helps with many different words.","Listening to a beginning Finnish lesson while reading its text makes this clear within about a minute."]},{"text":"**Doubled letters** mark a difference in length.","children":["A doubled consonant stands for a **geminated** consonant, which is a consonant held longer.","A doubled vowel stands for a **long vowel**."]},{"text":"**Finnish children** learn to **decode** very quickly. Decoding means producing a pronunciation from a written form.","children":["The matches are consistent, so children can apply them to written forms they have never seen."]},{"text":"So a transparent script makes decoding fast to learn.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p005":[{"text":"**English** is an **outlier** among languages that use the Roman alphabet. One result is that children learn to **decode** more slowly in English.","children":[]},{"text":"**Aro and Wimmer** compared children learning to read in several languages. The children read **pseudowords** aloud. Pseudowords are made-up words that follow the spelling and sound patterns of their language.","children":["A pseudoword is not a stored familiar word. So reading it tests decoding.","In **Grade 1**, children learning French, German, Dutch, Spanish, Swedish or Finnish read about 85 to 93 percent accurately.","In Grade 1, **English** learners read only about **half** of the pseudowords accurately.","English learners improve more slowly and reach about 88 percent by Grade 4."]},{"text":"English children still learn to decode, but they learn it more slowly. That is because English spelling-sound matches are less predictable. So English learners lag behind learners of the other languages in Grade 1.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p006":[{"text":"**Korean** writing, called **Hangul**, is even more regular than Finnish. In Hangul, the symbols for similar phonemes look similar to one another.","children":["Letters are grouped into **syllable blocks**.","Each block has an **initial** position, a **medial** position and sometimes a **final** position."]},{"text":"The written shapes reflect how the sounds are organized.","children":["For **velar** consonants, the shared feature is the basic angular shape ㄱ. Velar consonants are made with the back of the tongue against the soft palate.","The letter ㄱ is g, the letter ㄲ is kk and the letter ㅋ is k.","Doubling the shape gives kk. Adding a stroke gives k."]},{"text":"So the **angular ㄱ shape** corresponds roughly to velar place of articulation. That is why Hangul shows sound structure in the letter shapes themselves.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p007":[{"text":"Reading **pseudowords** aloud puts **decoding** into action. GLORP, SPLING, JONK, FEESH and QUISP are not English words.","children":[]},{"text":"A reader can still pronounce each one.","children":["Each item follows enough English spelling patterns to build a **plausible pronunciation**.","The reader has no stored pronunciation for these items. So success cannot come from memory of each word.","The reader uses knowledge that **generalizes** across words."]},{"text":"The items test decoding, not vocabulary or word meanings. So reading a new letter string reveals knowledge beyond memorized words.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p008":[{"text":"**Lupker, Brown and Colombo (1997)** had people read items aloud and measured **naming time**. Naming time is how quickly a pronunciation becomes available.","children":["They compared pseudowords, here called **nonwords**, with real words."]},{"text":"Nonwords were read **nearly as quickly and accurately** as real words.","children":["In this example, nonword naming times were only **5 to 12 percent slower** than for low-frequency words.","The comparison is with low-frequency words, not with all real words."]},{"text":"So people do decoding fairly **naturally**, **automatically** and **implicitly**.","children":["One open question is how decoding should be characterized.","Computational models of word reading ask whether it is best captured by **categorical rules** or **probabilistic constraints**."]},{"text":"Rules and probabilistic constraints could both produce fast decoding. That is why both kinds of model must explain fast nonword naming.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p009":[{"text":"Some pseudowords have **more than one plausible pronunciation**. VONTH, PHANGE, STREAD and SOUGH are examples.","children":[]},{"text":"Each item contains a spelling pattern that English pronounces in more than one way.","children":["A reader answers using spelling-sound patterns learned from familiar words.","Different familiar words can favor different answers for the same new item.","So these items have no single agreed pronunciation."]},{"text":"These items test how readers handle **ambiguity** in the mapping from spelling to sound. So generalization can be **graded** rather than all-or-none.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p010":[{"text":"**Generalization** is a good way to test what regularities people have learned about a system. A new item cannot have been memorized, so the answer reveals the learned pattern.","children":["The **Wug Test** is another example of testing generalization."]},{"text":"STREAD can be read as /strɛd/ or as /strid/.","children":["Readers accept **both** pronunciations.","The two readings differ only in the vowel. The rest of STREAD stays the same."]},{"text":"It is hard to imagine a **rule set** that covers this intuition.","children":["A **categorical rule** gives one output. So it must explain why two outputs feel acceptable.","A **statistical account** can give each pronunciation a different amount of support."]},{"text":"So accepting both readings reveals the kind of knowledge being generalized.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p011":[{"text":"The **regularity effect** is that **irregular** words are harder to recognize than **regular** words. Irregular words are also called **inconsistent** words.","children":["It is uncontroversial that some form of decoding is part of normal reading."]},{"text":"For some words the case is straightforward.","children":["PINT, ACHE and CHOIR do not follow the usual spelling-sound patterns. So decoding them by those patterns gives the wrong pronunciation."]},{"text":"For **neighborhoods** of words the case is less clear-cut. A neighborhood is a set of words that share a spelling pattern.","children":["In ROLL, POLL, TOLL and DOLL, three words share one pronunciation and DOLL differs.","In COOK, TOOK, BOOK and SPOOK, three words share one pronunciation and SPOOK differs."]},{"text":"So a word's difficulty depends partly on how its spelling neighbors are pronounced. That is why regularity is better treated as how much support a pronunciation gets from similar words.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p012":[{"text":"**Sublexical information** is information about units smaller than a whole word. Readers use it heavily, especially to translate spelling into sound.","children":["People read **nonwords** quickly and accurately.","Words that follow typical spelling-to-sound patterns are easier to read than words that do not."]},{"text":"**Lexical information** is knowledge of particular words.","children":["Nonwords test generalization from spelling patterns.","Differences among familiar words can reveal word-specific knowledge."]},{"text":"So general decoding explains only part of reading. That is why **lexical processes** also need an account.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p013":[{"text":"The **Interactive Activation Model** of **McClelland and Rumelhart (1981)** links features, letters and words. Visual features activate letters. Letters activate candidate words such as WEAR, WORK, WORD and WEAK.","children":["Active word candidates send **feedback** to the letters they contain."]},{"text":"In a word perception example, blots cover parts of the letters in WORK.","children":["The visible features of the last letter fit K and R, but not D.","Of those letters, only K makes a known word, WORK. So WORK sends feedback to K."]},{"text":"Over time, activation for **K** rises close to the maximum.","children":["Activation for **R** rises a little and then falls back.","Activation for **D** stays low."]},{"text":"So **word knowledge helps identify an ambiguous letter**. Recognition builds up over time rather than happening at once.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p014":[{"text":"In the **Interactive Activation Model**, **feedback** from known words raises activation for the letters in those words.","children":["A partly active word candidate sends activation back down to its letters.","Letter-to-word activation gives evidence for a word.","Word-to-letter feedback supports that word's letters."]},{"text":"So recognition is **interactive**. Information does not flow only upward from features to a finished word.","children":["Recognizing a word and recognizing its letters support each other."]},{"text":"That is why an ambiguous letter is **easier to recognize inside a familiar word**.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p015":[{"text":"Two **lexical effects** have already come up. Both reflect experience with particular words.","children":["The **lexicality effect** is that real words are named faster than nonwords.","The **word frequency effect** is that words met more often are named faster.","In one naming study, high-frequency words took about 442 ms and low-frequency words about 482 ms. Nonwords took about 541 to 552 ms."]},{"text":"As with **picture naming**, the number of past encounters with a word affects how fast it can be named.","children":[]},{"text":"**Balota and colleagues (2004)** used data from the **English Lexicon Project**.","children":["Naming time drops steadily as word frequency rises.","The effect is **robust** over a very large number of observations."]},{"text":"So familiarity with a particular word speeds naming, beyond what general spelling patterns give.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p016":[{"text":"Frequency effects might be **confounds** of word length and other variables. A confound is another variable that changes along with frequency and could cause the same effect.","children":["Frequent words could, for example, also be shorter."]},{"text":"A **multiple regression analysis** tested this. Multiple regression measures how much each predictor explains reaction time while the other predictors are included.","children":["The analysis used about 3000 words read by 30 young and older adults.","The tasks were **lexical decision** and **naming**. Lexical decision is judging whether a letter string is a real word.","Predictors included sound features, length, frequency, neighborhood size and consistency."]},{"text":"**Objective** and **subjective frequency** still predicted reaction time with the other variables included.","children":["Their coefficients were **negative** for both age groups and both tasks. So higher frequency went with **faster** responses."]},{"text":"So the frequency effect is **not just a confound** of length.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p017":[{"text":"The **frequency by regularity interaction** is a landmark finding in psycholinguistics. The **frequency effect** is much larger for **exception words** than for **regular words**.","children":["Exception words have a pronunciation that breaks the usual spelling pattern.","**Naming latency** is the time needed to begin saying a word."]},{"text":"For **high-frequency** words, exception and regular words are both named in about 540 ms. For **low-frequency** words, exception words take about 583 ms. Regular words take only about 557 ms.","children":[]},{"text":"An **interaction** means the effect of one factor depends on the other factor.","children":["The regularity cost is not a fixed amount added to every word.","It is small for frequent words and large for rare words."]},{"text":"So the finding means that experience matters most when pronunciation conflicts with the usual spelling pattern. It has influenced other areas, most immediately **inflectional morphology**.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p018":[{"text":"One interpretation says word recognition has **two separate processes**. This is the **dual-route** account.","children":["The **sublexical process** is decoding, which builds a pronunciation from spelling units.","The **lexical process** is knowledge of a particular word, which supplies its learned pronunciation."]},{"text":"Each kind of item depends on the processes differently.","children":["For **high-frequency (HF)** words, the lexical process is so fast and efficient that the sublexical process has little influence.","For **low-frequency (LF)** words, the lexical process is less efficient. So the sublexical process shows up.","For **nonwords**, there is no stored word. So the sublexical process shows up there too."]},{"text":"For an LF exception word, decoding gives a regularized pronunciation. That output competes with the correct lexical one, so naming slows.","children":[]},{"text":"So two routes explain why regularity matters most for less familiar words. This is one interpretation of the interaction, not the interaction itself.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p019":[{"text":"**Acquired phonological dyslexia** is reading difficulty caused by brain injury. It is called **acquired**, as opposed to **developmental** dyslexia, because it follows an injury.","children":["These patients have difficulty specifically with **pseudoword naming**."]},{"text":"**Cacciopo van Vliet and colleagues (2003)** tested two patients, M.O. and I.B. Both read real words and nonwords aloud.","children":["For **real words**, both patients were mostly correct, at about 85 to 100 percent.","This held for concrete and abstract words, for every word class and for high and low frequency.","For **nonwords**, plausible responses were much lower, often under half.","Nonwords with low **grapheme-to-phoneme correspondence** were hardest, at about 15 to 20 percent. A grapheme is a written unit that stands for a sound."]},{"text":"So **familiar-word reading can survive while decoding of new forms fails**. The deficit is relative, not total.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p020":[{"text":"**Acquired surface dyslexia** is also reading difficulty after brain injury. Here the difficulty is specifically with **irregular words**.","children":["The mispronunciations tend to be **regularizations**. A regularization reads a word by the common spelling-sound pattern."]},{"text":"DEAF is read as “deef”, SAID as “sayed” and BROAD as “brode”.","children":["SHOE is read as “show”, ONE as “own” and FLOOD as “flude”.","GONE is read as “goan”, STEAK as “steek”, SEW as “sue” and SOOT as “suit”."]},{"text":"Two patients, MP and KT, read regular words, exception words and nonwords.","children":["For **regular words**, both were accurate, at about 89 to 100 percent.","For **nonwords**, both were accurate, at about 95 to 100 percent.","For **exception words**, accuracy dropped most for low-frequency items. KT scored 47 percent on high-frequency and 26 percent on low-frequency exceptions. MP scored 93 and 73 percent."]},{"text":"So the errors are **systematic**. The patients apply typical patterns to words that are exceptions.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p021":[{"text":"Two patient groups show **opposite patterns** after brain injury. This is a classic **double dissociation**.","children":[]},{"text":"In **acquired surface dyslexia**, the difficulty is specifically with irregular words.","children":["The mispronunciations tend to be regularizations.","**Pseudoword reading** is relatively spared."]},{"text":"In **acquired phonological dyslexia**, the difficulty is specifically with pseudoword naming.","children":["**Exception word reading** is relatively spared."]},{"text":"In a double dissociation, one group is impaired on one task but not another. The other group shows the reverse.","children":["Relatively spared does not mean perfect."]},{"text":"So the two patterns together are hard to explain by saying one task is simply harder for everyone.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p022":[{"text":"Double dissociations are powerful because the **impairment reverses** across patients. Suppose task A were just harder than task B. Then damage would hurt A more in every patient.","children":["That could not explain another patient who shows the reverse pattern."]},{"text":"In reading, **phonological dyslexia** hurts pseudowords more. **Surface dyslexia** hurts exception words more.","children":["So pseudowords and exception words place different demands on the reading system."]},{"text":"The reversal points to **separable processes** behind the two tasks.","children":["First, each patient's relative costs are compared. Then the two profiles are compared.","Two low scores without an opposite pattern are not a double dissociation."]},{"text":"So the evidence is strong because it rules out a single ranking of difficulty. It still needs a theory of what the processes are.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p023":[{"text":"Double dissociations provide evidence that the processes behind **pseudoword** and **irregular word** reading are **separable**. **Morton's logogen model**, from **Morton and Patterson (1980)**, locates where damage could occur.","children":["**Logogens** are stored word units. Visual input logogens recognize written words. Output logogens supply their spoken forms.","**Grapheme-phoneme conversion** turns letters into sounds without using a stored word."]},{"text":"In this model, each dyslexia comes from different damage.","children":["**Phonological dyslexia** results from specific damage to the **grapheme-phoneme conversion module**. Nonwords have no stored word, so they are read poorly.","**Surface dyslexia** results from damage to **input or output logogens**, or the connections between them. Exception words then rely on conversion, so they come out regularized."]},{"text":"These are theoretical components, not a map of brain regions. So the model turns the observed dissociation into claims about damaged components.","children":[]}],"m3-reading1-p024":[{"text":"**Patterson (1981)** gave early hints that the dual-route account is **not so simple**. In that account, a word is read either from its stored whole-word pronunciation or by **non-lexical grapheme-phoneme translation**.","children":["Surface dyslexia errors were thought to come from the non-lexical route. So they should show no lexical influences."]},{"text":"**Marcel (1980)** looked closely at the error data and found several **lexical influences**.","children":["**Neologisms**, which are made-up non-words, occur less often than a purely non-lexical process predicts.","One patient read incense as “increase”. Part of the difficulty was the ambiguous consonant c.","If that were the only factor, the patient should have produced the neologism “inkense”."]},{"text":"This poses a **classic dilemma** with two options.","children":["One option changes the view of surface dyslexia. It would reflect a more complicated combination of the two routes.","The other option changes the view of non-lexical reading. It would be a more complicated route."]},{"text":"Models of normal reading ask the same question about non-words, as in **Glushko (1979)**. So Patterson argued that combining normal and patient evidence holds the best promise.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p001":[],"m3-reading2-p002":[{"text":"**Dual-route models** of visual word recognition propose two separate routes for reading. Six reading findings motivate this kind of model.","children":["**Pseudoword reading** is fast and automatic.","Word reading is faster than pseudoword reading.","Reading is fastest for **high-frequency words**.","**Regularity** also affects accuracy and response time.","**Frequency and regularity interact** with each other.","In **acquired dyslexia**, pseudoword reading and irregular word reading are **dissociable**. Either one can be impaired while the other stays intact."]},{"text":"So the model hypothesizes two routes.","children":["The **sublexical route** does **grapheme-to-phoneme conversion**, also called **decoding**. A grapheme is a written unit that stands for a sound.","The **lexical route** recognizes real words by using knowledge of particular words."]},{"text":"The dissociation in dyslexia carries the most weight. Two abilities that can be impaired independently suggest two independent processes. That is why the model has one route for decoding and one route for known words.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p003":[{"text":"**Coltheart and colleagues (2001)** built the **Dual Route Cascaded model**, called the **DRC**. It joins an interactive lexical route to a separate rule route.","children":[]},{"text":"Three design points matter.","children":["The lexical part of the DRC is just the **Interactive Activation model**.","The DRC has **no implementation of semantics**. The **orthographic lexicon** holds written word forms. The **phonological lexicon** holds spoken word forms. Both lexicons are independent of meaning.","The **Grapheme-Phoneme Rule System** has completely different processing assumptions from the interactive part."]},{"text":"Both routes send activation toward a spoken response. The rule system joins the output instead of becoming another interactive layer.","children":[]},{"text":"So the DRC uses two different kinds of computation. That is why it counts as two routes and not one network.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p004":[{"text":"The **Grapheme-Phoneme Rule System** in the DRC has four important features.","children":["Its rules are activated **serially**, letter by letter.","It is **not affected by frequency** of occurrence in any way.","It has **no feedback**.","It **does not interact** with the lexical route, except at one place."]},{"text":"That place is the **phoneme response buffer**. Both routes connect to the same output units there.","children":["Because they share output units, the two routes can **compete** to activate different patterns.","For an exception word, the lexical route favors the correct pronunciation. The rule route favors a regularized pronunciation. The two pronunciations compete in the buffer."]},{"text":"So the routes stay separate inside but meet at the output. That is why competing pronunciations can affect naming even without feedback into the rules.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p005":[{"text":"**GPC rules** map written units onto phonemes. Coltheart and colleagues list them as the DRC's nonlexical rules.","children":["**Multiletter rules** convert a group of letters into one phoneme. Examples include eigh, ough, tsch and ph.","**Context-sensitive rules** convert a grapheme only when certain letters come before or after it. For instance, c becomes s before e.","Each rule has a **position**. It may apply at all positions, at the beginning, in the middle or at the end."]},{"text":"A list of rules is not enough by itself. The system also needs **meta-rules** for when to apply rules at different levels of analysis.","children":["One meta-rule says that **larger-unit rules supersede smaller-unit rules**. So a multiletter rule wins over the rules for its single letters."]},{"text":"So the GPC procedure is more than one sound per letter. It needs both the rules and an order for using them.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p006":[{"text":"The GPC rules are **not totally independent of statistics**. Each rule is often based on the **most frequent pronunciation** for a letter or group of letters.","children":[]},{"text":"But the rules are **fixed and categorical**. A rule either applies or it does not, with no strength attached.","children":["For **EIGH**, /eI/ has a proportion of 0.667 and /aI/ has 0.333. When the final letter is not T, /eI/ rises to 1.","For **OUGH**, support is spread over five pronunciations. /ɔ/ is most common at 0.53. /әf/ and /oʊ/ follow at 0.16 each.","When the final letter is not T, /ɔ/ and /әf/ tie at 0.27 for OUGH.","So the OUGH rule has **weaker statistical support** than the EIGH rule."]},{"text":"Even so, both are rules, and they **behave the same way during processing**. Statistics help choose each rule. Graded statistics play no part while reading.","children":[]},{"text":"That is why the DRC treats a weak rule and a strong rule identically.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p007":[{"text":"The DRC's GPC rules are kind of silly, because they **seem to miss a lot of cases**. Its designers saw this as a **feature, not a bug**.","children":[]},{"text":"Better rules would be possible.","children":["Rules could take statistics into account more flexibly.","The DRC's rules did go through several revisions to improve its **nonword reading**."]},{"text":"The DRC argues that people **do not use finely tuned rules** for the GPC route.","children":["If people did, there would be no pattern of **regularization errors** in **surface dyslexia**. A regularization error gives an exception word the pronunciation its spelling rules predict.","There would also be no **slow-down for low-frequency exception words**."]},{"text":"So the simple rules are meant to explain human errors as well as correct reading. That is why a rule set that misses cases can be the better model of people.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p008":[{"text":"**Informational encapsulation** means a process does not use every source of relevant knowledge. It works only with its own kind of information.","children":[]},{"text":"Models of syntax used the same idea.","children":["The parsing strategies **late closure** and **minimal attachment** were motivated by syntactic complexity only.","The aim was to explain syntactic processing as encapsulated from world knowledge and lexical co-occurrence statistics."]},{"text":"The dual-route models are designed to be **modular** in the same way.","children":["The GPC route chugs along without lexical feedback. It produces correct pronunciations for many words.","It produces incorrect responses to some **exceptions** to its rules."]},{"text":"The GPC route's operation can be observed only occasionally.","children":["It appears when people produce **regularization errors** on exception words.","It appears when people are made to **name pseudowords**, which have no stored entry."]},{"text":"So encapsulation predicts errors even when another part of the system knows the right answer.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p009":[{"text":"The DRC can **simulate many phenomena** in visual word recognition. **Coltheart and colleagues (2001)** listed them by task.","children":["In **reading aloud**, it simulates frequency, lexicality, regularity and consistency effects. It also simulates the interaction of regularity with frequency.","In **lexical decision**, it simulates the word frequency effect and the pseudohomophone effect, among others.","In **acquired dyslexias**, it simulates patterns in surface dyslexia and phonological dyslexia."]},{"text":"The authors relied on past reading research instead of backpropagation for ideas about the architecture. They took the same view as **Grainger and Jacobs (1998)**.","children":["That view holds that **human behavioral data** are currently the major source of constraint on models of cognition."]},{"text":"So the DRC is judged on how many human findings one architecture reproduces. Which assumptions stay fixed across tasks also matters.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p010":[{"text":"Modeling **lexical decision** in the DRC sometimes means **going to extremes** to make contact with data. Lexical decision is deciding whether a written item is a word.","children":[]},{"text":"First, the model **reduces letter-word inhibition**. This change makes the task harder. Without it, the results make no sense.","children":[]},{"text":"The model answers **yes** by either of two criteria.","children":["If any single word has activity above **0.69**, it answers yes.","If total activity over the **orthographic input lexicon** exceeds a threshold called **S**, it answers yes.","S is 10 from 0 to 20 cycles. It drops to 1.98 if lexicon activity exceeds 0.2 at 20 cycles."]},{"text":"The model answers **no** if neither criterion is met by a deadline called **D**.","children":["D is 42 cycles, unless lexicon activity exceeds 0.112 after 20 cycles. In that case D is set to 48.","This deadline is nearly twice as fast as the model normally reads aloud."]},{"text":"So the decision rules add many assumptions beyond the two routes. That is why this fit counts as going to extremes.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p011":[{"text":"The key feature of the DRC is **modularity**. The GPC route and the lexical route are modular in three ways.","children":["Each route has its **own processing assumptions**.","The routes are **informationally encapsulated** from one another. So neither route feeds information into the other.","The routes are **domain-specific**. The GPC rules can only take letters as input. They can only pass phoneme activations to the phoneme system."]},{"text":"These are three separate claims. One concerns how each route computes. One concerns how the routes communicate. One concerns what input each route accepts.","children":[]},{"text":"So modularity is more than having two pathways. That is why two arrows in a model do not make it modular by themselves.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p012":[{"text":"**Seidenberg and McClelland (1989)** took an **emergentist approach** to visual word recognition with a **PDP model**. In this approach, reading skill **emerges from learning**.","children":[]},{"text":"They saw reading skill as mappings among three kinds of information.","children":["**Orthography** is the spelling of words.","**Phonology** is pronunciation, or sound structure.","**Semantics** is the meaning of words."]},{"text":"They modeled how these mappings are learned.","children":["They trained a **simple associative network**.","They studied the mapping from **orthography to phonology** in a **single route**. Every kind of word used that one mapping."]},{"text":"So reading is not a hand-built list of rules and word entries in this model. It is what the network learns from experience with many words.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p013":[{"text":"The PDP model of **Seidenberg and McClelland (1989)** makes **different assumptions** from dual-route models.","children":[]},{"text":"Two of its assumptions differ.","children":["**Rules** are just **generalizations** from lots of words. They are not a separate process.","The **lexicon** is **distributed**. Some whole-word information is stored in the orthography to phonology mapping. The rest is stored in other parts of the system."]},{"text":"The **research strategy** differs too.","children":["PDP models explain reading with **domain-general learning mechanisms**. These are learning mechanisms not special to reading.","Dual-route models instead **reverse-engineer** the system from adult and neuropsychological data."]},{"text":"So one learned system holds both shared patterns and facts about single words. Distributed does not mean the model has no memory of individual words.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p014":[{"text":"The PDP model captured the **frequency by regularity interaction**. It did this **without** assuming that the two effects come from **different processes**.","children":[]},{"text":"The model answers how in three steps.","children":["**Regular words** are consistent with statistical patterns from many other words. So they get support from those words.","**Exception words** are inconsistent with these statistics. So they lack that support.","**Frequency** affects how well each word is learned. So highly frequent words are **less affected** by inconsistency with other words."]},{"text":"The hardest case is a **low-frequency exception word**. It has weak learning of its own. It also lacks support from other words.","children":[]},{"text":"So both effects come from one learning mechanism. That is why they interact without a separate rule system.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p015":[{"text":"PDP models **redefine regularity** as **consistency**. Consistency depends on a word's **friends** and **enemies**. Friends are similar spellings with the same pronunciation. Enemies are similar spellings with a different pronunciation.","children":[]},{"text":"The four categories run from most support to least.","children":["**Regular consistent** words have many friends and no enemies, like LATE and DUST.","**Regular inconsistent** words have many friends but at least one enemy. GAVE has the enemy HAVE. MINT has the enemy PINT.","**Ambiguous** words have about equal numbers of friends and enemies. Examples are DOWN and KNOWN, and POUR and SOUR.","**Exception** words have few if any friends and many enemies. HAVE has the enemy GAVE. PINT has the enemy MINT."]},{"text":"So spelling-to-sound support comes in degrees. A plain regular or irregular label hides those degrees.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p016":[{"text":"A PDP model reads **words and nonwords** in the **same route**. In principle, this works in two ways.","children":["**Nonwords** are named with the **same set of weights** that the network learned for words.","**Generalization** uses the same statistical patterns that give rise to **regularity effects**."]},{"text":"A new spelling overlaps with familiar spellings. So the network can pronounce it without a stored entry for that nonword.","children":[]},{"text":"So nonword reading does not require a separate GPC rule module. That claim still has to be checked against how the model actually performs.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p017":[{"text":"In practice, the PDP model's **nonword accuracy was not very good**. This is a **classic case of ambiguity in model evaluation**.","children":[]},{"text":"The failure has two possible causes.","children":["The **theoretical claim** might be wrong. That claim is that nonwords and exceptions are read by the same **associative mechanism**.","Or the problem might be **specific to how the model was made**."]},{"text":"The evidence points to the second cause.","children":["The model's **phonological representations**, called **Wickelfeatures**, were poor.","A later model with better phonological representations read nonwords better."]},{"text":"So a failed simulation does not reveal its own cause. That is why improving the implementation while keeping the theory helps tell the two causes apart.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p018":[{"text":"**Nonword reading** is related to **phonological representations**. Many studies find a link between **decoding**, meaning nonword reading, and **phonological processing**.","children":[]},{"text":"Seidenberg and McClelland used odd phonological representations called **Wickelfeatures**. These represented the sound structure of words poorly.","children":[]},{"text":"**Plaut and colleagues (1996)** improved the phonological representations. Their model produced **better performance on nonwords**.","children":[]},{"text":"So part of the early failure came from the representation, not the theory. That is why decoding improved without adding a rule system.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p019":[{"text":"**Generalization depends on similarity**. This principle is inherited from **behaviorism**.","children":[]},{"text":"Poor phonological representations, like **Wickelfeatures**, fail to capture the **similarity space** of words in the language. So the network cannot generalize well to new forms.","children":[]},{"text":"Words and nonwords suffer differently.","children":["**Word-specific information** is more robust to poor representations. It does not depend entirely on generalization.","A **nonword** has no word-specific information. So it depends much more on generalization."]},{"text":"So poor phonology hurts nonwords more than words. That is why a nonword deficit can arise without a separate nonword mechanism.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p020":[{"text":"**Harm and Seidenberg (1999)** studied this link more systematically. They manipulated the amount of **noise** in the model's phonological representations. More noise makes the sound representations less reliable.","children":[]},{"text":"The noisy models replicated key findings on **developmental dyslexia**.","children":["The degree of **phonological deficit** was related to the degree of **phonological dyslexia**. More noise went with worse nonword decoding.","**Word recognition** was **relatively spared**."]},{"text":"Noise was the property they changed. Word and nonword reading were the outcomes.","children":[]},{"text":"So a reading deficit that looks selective can come from a weak general phonological system. That is why nonwords, which rely on generalization, suffer most.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p021":[{"text":"The PDP account also has to explain **double dissociations**. Nonword reading can already be specifically impaired without a specific nonword reading mechanism.","children":[]},{"text":"**Plaut and colleagues (1996)** explored the other end of the dissociation, **surface dyslexia**. Surface dyslexia is a specific impairment on exception words.","children":["They implemented a **simplified semantic system**.","They **lesioned** semantics, meaning they damaged that part of the model.","The lesion caused a **specific impairment on exception words**."]},{"text":"Exception words get little support from typical spelling-to-sound patterns. So they rely more on support from meaning.","children":[]},{"text":"So damage to semantics produces surface dyslexia in the model. That is why no separate exception-word mechanism is needed.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p022":[{"text":"The triangle model is **not just the dual-route model renamed**. Three differences explain why.","children":["**Semantic representations** are directly involved. The DRC instead commits to a lexicon independent of semantics.","A later model by **Harm and Seidenberg (2004)** implemented featural semantics. It also simulated the mapping of spelling to meaning.","The **OP** (orthography to phonology) and **SP** (semantics to phonology) mappings share **processing assumptions** and a learning algorithm. Both mappings are **probabilistic**.","The pathways **interact over learning**. The OP pathway develops differently next to an OSP pathway than it would alone."]},{"text":"This interactivity and gradedness explain why double dissociations are **never entirely clean**.","children":["**Phonological dyslexics** are not perfect on exception words.","**Surface dyslexics** are not perfect on nonwords."]},{"text":"So multiple pathways do not make a model modular. That is why the triangle model and the DRC stay different accounts.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p023":[{"text":"**Woollams and colleagues (2007)** tested a prediction of the triangle model. The model predicts a **strong association** between **semantic abilities** and **exception word reading**.","children":[]},{"text":"They compared model runs with patients.","children":["In the models, weaker semantic activation went with lower accuracy on **low-frequency exception words**. The fit was strong, with an R squared of 0.84.","In patients with **semantic dementia**, lower semantic scores went with lower exception word accuracy. The fit was weaker, with an R squared of 0.50."]},{"text":"So poorer semantics goes with poorer exception reading in both models and patients. Individual patients still vary around the trend.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p024":[{"text":"The triangle model **misses a few data points**. A few patients are **statistical outliers**.","children":["Some patients have unusually high semantic scores.","Some patients have unusually high exception word accuracy, even with lower semantic scores."]},{"text":"Woollams and colleagues say what to make of this. They would treat these patients **the way outliers are normally treated** when evaluating a model's fit to data.","children":[]},{"text":"A graded association does not predict zero variability. So a few outliers do not refute the model by themselves.","children":[]},{"text":"The real question is whether the outliers show a stable separate pattern. Repeated testing of the same patients addresses that question.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p025":[{"text":"Woollams and colleagues studied a **progressive condition**, **semantic dementia**. So they could follow **exception word reading** as **semantic abilities declined**.","children":[]},{"text":"In most cases, a patient's **reading ability declined** when her **semantic abilities declined** over time.","children":[]},{"text":"Repeated observations also showed **regression to the mean**. Unusual first scores moved closer to the general trend on later tests.","children":["If the dissociation between semantics and exception reading came from **idiosyncratic outliers**, it would show just this pattern."]},{"text":"So repeated testing strengthens the association. That is why a one-time unusual score is weak evidence against the triangle model.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p026":[{"text":"**Coltheart and colleagues (2009)** simulated the same surface dyslexia data with the **DRC**.","children":[]},{"text":"They generated **40,000 model patients**. These varied on two parameters.","children":["One parameter was the **integrity of the lexical route**.","The other parameter was the **integrity of the GPC rules**."]},{"text":"For each patient in the Woollams data, they **selected the model run** that best fit that patient.","children":[]},{"text":"This produced an **excellent fit**. Patient and model accuracy on low-frequency exception words stayed close as semantic impairment grew more severe.","children":[]},{"text":"So the selection step is part of how the fit was achieved. That is why this fit differs from predicting each patient in advance.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p027":[{"text":"A better fit can be a **bad thing** when it comes from **flexibility**. The DRC fit was chosen from 40,000 model patients after seeing each patient's data.","children":[]},{"text":"The two models reached their fits differently.","children":["The **DRC** picked the best match for each patient from many parameter settings. So it accommodated each case after the outcome.","The **triangle model** predicted the association between semantics and exception reading."]},{"text":"Close agreement is not bad in itself. A flexible model can match many possible patterns. So its match does not strongly constrain the explanation.","children":[]},{"text":"That is why goodness of fit is only one part of evaluating a model.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p028":[{"text":"**Dual-route** and **triangle models** both cover a range of reading data. They share four basic facts.","children":["**Decoding** is fast and automatic, as in pseudoword reading.","Words that diverge from typical spelling-to-sound mappings are **harder to read**.","This **regularity** or **consistency effect** interacts with **word frequency**.","Pseudoword reading and exception or inconsistent word reading are **dissociable** in neuropsychological patients."]},{"text":"The models agree on these facts but not on mechanisms. Dual-route models use specialized routes. Triangle models use learning across interacting systems.","children":[]},{"text":"So the contest is over explanation, not over whether the facts exist.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p029":[{"text":"The two models give **different explanations** for the same four phenomena.","children":[]},{"text":"For **pseudoword reading and regularity effects**, the **triangle** model uses statistical regularities learned over many words. The **dual-route** model uses a dedicated **GPC rule system**.","children":[]},{"text":"For **frequency effects**, the triangle model says more exposure to a word gives better learning for that word. The dual-route model uses distinct input and output lexicons, separate from the GPC rules.","children":[]},{"text":"For **phonological dyslexia**, the triangle model points to a deficit in **phonological processing** generally. The dual-route model points to damage in the **GPC route**.","children":[]},{"text":"For **surface dyslexia**, the triangle model points to a deficit in **semantic processes** generally. The dual-route model points to damage in the **lexical route**.","children":[]},{"text":"So each finding stays the same while its proposed cause differs.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p030":[{"text":"The **key contrast** is between **dissociations** and **associations**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Dual-route models** are generally motivated by **double dissociations**. Pseudowords and exception words can be impaired independently. So they should be modeled as independent processes.","children":[]},{"text":"Models informed by **statistical learning** instead reveal **associations**.","children":["**Phonology** is important for learning and using orthography to phonology generalizations. So phonological damage hurts pseudoword reading.","**Semantics** is important for words where that generalization is ambiguous. So semantic damage hurts exception words."]},{"text":"So the triangle account ties each dyslexia to a broader language ability. That is why phonological and semantic measures help explain reading deficits.","children":[]}],"m3-reading2-p031":[],"m3-morphology-p001":[],"m3-morphology-p002":[{"text":"**Morphology** is **generative**, which means speakers can form words they have not memorized one by one.","children":[]},{"text":"**Derivational morphology** turns one kind of word into another kind.","children":["English has a process for turning verbs into nouns.","At some point there was no noun \"generative,\" only the verb \"to generate.\"","The derivational process created the new word."]},{"text":"**Inflectional morphology** marks grammatical information on a word.","children":["In English, inflection is mostly the **plural** and the **past tense**.","These two give clear cases of a speaker extending a pattern to a new item."]},{"text":"Generalizing can also produce playful forms.","children":["One person wrote \"She found me crying and she crew too and we both crode.\"","The forms crew and crode extend irregular-sounding patterns to cry.","They are creative forms, not standard English past tenses."]},{"text":"So speakers produce forms they never heard, and the open question is whether that needs symbolic rules or can come from learning.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p003":[{"text":"**Jean Berko Gleason (1958)** ran a classic study called the **wug test**. It measures how readily people generalize an inflection to a new word.","children":[]},{"text":"A child sees a drawing of a made-up creature.","children":["The text under it says \"This is a WUG.\"","Then a second creature appears, and the text says \"Now there is another one. There are two of them.\"","The text ends with \"There are two\" and a blank for the child to fill.","Saying **WUGS** applies plural knowledge to a new noun."]},{"text":"The word is new, so the child cannot have memorized its plural.","children":["The child must extend a learned pattern instead.","The same logic tests the **past tense** with made-up verbs.","Adults generalize readily, and children do this too."]},{"text":"Success means the child has **productive knowledge** of the pattern.","children":[]},{"text":"So people readily extend inflection to words they have never heard.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p004":[{"text":"The English past tense has **regular** and **irregular** forms.","children":[]},{"text":"**Regular** verbs add **-ed**.","children":["Real verbs follow this pattern, as in show-showed, bake-baked and wade-waded.","Made-up verbs follow it too, as in blick-blicked, trog-trogged and wumble-wumbled.","The made-up verbs test whether the pattern extends to new words."]},{"text":"**Irregular** verbs do not follow the -ed pattern.","children":["Examples are go-went, take-took, eat-ate and sleep-slept.","Each irregular has to be handled as an exception.","Some irregulars share smaller patterns with other verbs."]},{"text":"This split echoes the **dual-route model** of reading.","children":["That model also separated a general pattern from word-specific knowledge."]},{"text":"So a theory of the past tense must explain both productive extension and familiar exceptions.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p005":[{"text":"**Pinker and colleagues** proposed the **Words and Rules** account. It treats words and rules as **separate processes** with separate representational formats.","children":[]},{"text":"For **regular** verbs, a **symbolic rule** makes the past tense.","children":["The rule is something like \"add -ed.\"","The rule needs no stored entry for each past tense.","So the rule can apply to a brand-new verb."]},{"text":"For **irregular** verbs, the past tense is **lexicalized** within an **associative network**.","children":["Lexicalized means the form is stored as part of that word's own entry.","The network holds word-specific information.","Frequency and similarity can influence it."]},{"text":"The account also has to stop a stored irregular from being replaced by a regularization.","children":[]},{"text":"So in this account, productive rule use and remembered exceptions are two separate kinds of knowledge.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p006":[{"text":"**Pinker and Ullman (2002)** proposed a **dual-route model** for the past tense. The input is a word stem, such as walk or hold, plus a grammatical feature, such as past tense.","children":[]},{"text":"The **lexicon** route looks up a stored form.","children":["For hold, it retrieves held.","Its computation is lookup and association.","It is used for roots, idioms, irregulars and some regulars.","It belongs to **declarative memory**, which holds words and facts.","Its principal substrate is temporo-parietal cortex."]},{"text":"The **grammar** route combines pieces.","children":["For walk, it combines walk with the suffix -ed.","Its computation is combination and unification.","It is used for phrases, sentences and any regular form.","It belongs to the **procedural system**, which holds rules and skills.","Its principal substrate is frontal cortex and basal ganglia."]},{"text":"A **blocking** connection runs from the lexicon to the grammar.","children":["A retrieved irregular stops the regular form from competing."]},{"text":"The model is **similar** to the dual-route model of reading.","children":["Both separate word-specific knowledge from a productive operation."]},{"text":"The model is **different** in its task and its origins.","children":["Reading converts spelling into sound.","The past-tense model changes a stem according to a grammatical feature.","It came from grammar theory, not from patient dissociations."]},{"text":"So two models can look alike while solving different problems.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p007":[{"text":"The past-tense model and the reading model look alike, but they have **different origins**.","children":[]},{"text":"The **dual-route model of reading** was built on neuropsychological **double dissociations**.","children":["A double dissociation means each of two abilities can be damaged while the other is spared."]},{"text":"**Pinker and colleagues** were motivated by other concerns.","children":["**Generative linguistics** of the time was mostly about **categorical, symbolic, rule-based grammars**.","These rules often have **exceptions**.","The Words and Rules account keeps the rule system apart from the exceptions."]},{"text":"So the reading model came from patient data, while the past-tense model came from a theory of grammar.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p008":[{"text":"Linguists often just left exceptions out of the grammar. Adding machinery to handle them would make the grammar **inelegant**.","children":[]},{"text":"The **dual-process model** proposes a way to handle the exceptions.","children":["It keeps the grammar **elegant, symbolic and categorical**.","It puts the exceptions into a second process."]},{"text":"The second process has all the properties the rule route does not have.","children":["It is **word-specific**, or lexical.","It is **sensitive to frequency** of occurrence.","It is **sensitive to phonological similarity**, as in keep-kept, weep-wept and leap-leapt.","Those verbs share a sound pattern, so an exception can belong to a small family."]},{"text":"So separating the exceptions keeps the rule simple, but it adds a second mechanism that must also be explained.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p009":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland (1987)** proposed an alternative. In their model, the past tense is all **associative learning**.","children":[]},{"text":"One trainable network learns both regular and irregular verbs.","children":["There is no separate \"add -ed\" rule.","Regularity is a pattern learned from many examples.","Irregular forms are learned in the same system."]},{"text":"The model has three parts.","children":["A **fixed encoding network** turns the phonological representation of the root form into a **Wickelfeature** representation.","A **pattern associator** with **modifiable connections** maps root Wickelfeatures onto past-tense Wickelfeatures.","A **decoding and binding network** turns those features into the phonological representation of the past tense.","Wickelfeatures represent sound patterns. The modifiable connections store the learned mapping."]},{"text":"So one learning mechanism has to explain both the regular pattern and the exceptions.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p010":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland** aimed their model at developmental data. It was one of the first learning models of a language process.","children":[]},{"text":"The target was **U-shaped learning** of the past tense.","children":["First, children learn some frequent irregular verbs quite early, such as run-ran.","Then, spontaneously, children start producing **regularizations** such as runned.","A regularization applies the regular -ed pattern to an irregular verb.","Eventually, children produce the correct forms again."]},{"text":"Learning is called U-shaped because children get worse before they get better.","children":["Performance goes down, then goes back up."]},{"text":"So a theory must explain the whole path of development, not just the final adult form.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p011":[{"text":"U-shaped learning matters to the **Words and Rules** account because each part of the U is a **distinct stage** with a distinct process.","children":[]},{"text":"In the first stage, early correct forms are just **imitations**.","children":["They do not depend on the same mechanisms as adult processing.","A child can say ran without having any rule."]},{"text":"In the second stage, the child learns the rule.","children":["The rule is applied to all cases.","The rule is the child's **best hypothesis** for all items generally.","So the child now says runned."]},{"text":"In the third stage, exceptions are learned **associatively**, one by one.","children":["They become part of **lexical knowledge**.","The **blocking process** also develops. Blocking means a retrieved irregular stops the regular form.","So ran blocks runned, and the child is correct again."]},{"text":"That is why this account reads the dip in the U as rule use that exceptions do not yet hold back.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p012":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland's** model also produces **U-shaped learning**. It does this with one network and no rule.","children":[]},{"text":"The **training set** is based on data about input to children.","children":["That input changes over time."]},{"text":"Early on, **irregular verbs** predominate.","children":["The highest-frequency verbs tend to have irregular past tenses, such as GIVE, TAKE, HAVE and GO.","So the model learns these irregulars early."]},{"text":"Later, the vocabulary expands to include more regular verbs.","children":["The model **reorganizes** to capture those regularities.","This **interferes** with some early-learned irregulars."]},{"text":"For high-frequency irregular verbs, correct features dip briefly early in training.","children":["Regular verbs climb quickly and stay high.","The irregulars recover and climb more slowly."]},{"text":"Response strength tells the same story.","children":["Early on, the regularized form briefly beats the correct irregular form.","With more training, the correct form wins and the regularized form fades."]},{"text":"So a change in the input can make one network get worse before it gets better.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p013":[{"text":"**Generalization testing** checks whether the model learned structure it can transfer to new verbs.","children":[]},{"text":"The model was trained on a small number of verbs.","children":["So many real English verbs were new to the model.","That let the researchers run a **wug test** with real words."]},{"text":"The model received unfamiliar low-frequency regular verbs.","children":["Many responses were correct regular forms, such as shaped, sipped and typed.","Other responses were odd, such as stepted for step, squawked for squat and membled for mail."]},{"text":"The model generated most **Wickelfeatures** correctly.","children":["The proportion correct was .92 for regulars and .84 for irregulars.","It was .91 overall.","Correct features do not guarantee that every whole word came out right."]},{"text":"So the model generalized well at the level of features, but whole words could still go wrong.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p014":[{"text":"Some model outputs changed consonants in ways people would not.","children":[]},{"text":"Just four responses added or changed consonants.","children":["The model gave maded as the past tense of mate.","It gave squawked for squat.","It gave membled for mail.","It gave toureder for tour."]},{"text":"The model's authors said humans are unlikely to make these errors, especially the last two.","children":["They noted that most of these responses were **near threshold**, meaning only weakly produced.","They suggested adding an **auto-associative network** among the output units.","Such a network could clean up the output pattern and avoid bizarre responses.","They had not yet built it."]},{"text":"So matching the learning curve is not enough when the model makes words people would never produce.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p015":[{"text":"**Prince and Pinker (1988)** criticized the model for **blending** outputs.","children":[]},{"text":"The model uses **feature-only representations**.","children":["With features alone, representing two things at once is a problem.","In vision, features alone cannot tell a green circle near a red square from a red circle near a green square."]},{"text":"In the Rumelhart and McClelland model, subregularities and the regular rule compete.","children":["They all try to activate Wickelfeatures for the past form.","When sing is input, features for sing, sang, sung, singed and sanged can all be active at once.","The model does not put out a single word.","It puts out an unordered collection of features, many of them contradictory."]},{"text":"So the model cannot keep competing outputs apart.","children":["It blends them into bizarre hybrids.","Examples are toureder as the past of tour and membled for mail."]},{"text":"That is why Prince and Pinker located the problem in the representation itself.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p016":[{"text":"**Joanisse and Seidenberg (1999)** built a newer model of the past tense.","children":[]},{"text":"One system links **speech input**, **semantics** and **speech output**.","children":["Speech input and semantics both connect through a middle layer to speech output."]},{"text":"Their theoretical premise has two parts.","children":["**Rule-like generalization** is supported by **phonological representations**, which are the sound forms of words.","**Irregular** forms require greater support from **semantics**, which is word meaning."]},{"text":"So regulars and irregulars differ in how much they rely on sound and meaning, not in using separate routes.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p017":[{"text":"**Joanisse and Seidenberg (1999)** aimed their model at a **dissociation** in patient data.","children":[]},{"text":"Some patients are worse at **irregular** past tenses.","children":["Other patients are worse at **nonword** past tenses."]},{"text":"The model has to produce both patterns from one system.","children":["A nonword has no stored meaning, so it relies on phonological generalization.","An irregular is poorly predicted by the main pattern, so it relies more on semantics.","Damaging phonology or semantics should then give opposite impairment profiles."]},{"text":"So the test is whether damage to one shared system can explain both patient groups.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p018":[{"text":"**Broca's aphasia** comes from injury to **left anterior** brain regions.","children":[]},{"text":"Patients have **poor speech production**.","children":[]},{"text":"They have problems with **regular morphology**, especially **nonwords**.","children":["Nonword examples are wug and wugged, and blick and blicked.","A nonword has no stored past tense, so it must be produced by generalizing."]},{"text":"Their accuracy follows a clear order.","children":["Irregulars are about 85 percent correct.","Regulars are about 82 percent correct.","Nonwords are about 69 percent correct.","The nonword drop is much larger than the gap between irregulars and regulars."]},{"text":"So these patients seem to have trouble with productive inflection, which raises the question of a phonological cause.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p019":[{"text":"**Joanisse and Seidenberg** damaged the **phonological** part of their model.","children":[]},{"text":"The damage **reduced** the model's ability to compute past-tense forms.","children":[]},{"text":"The model's pattern matched the Broca's patients.","children":["**Nonwords** were worse than regulars and irregulars. Model nonwords were about 72 percent correct, and patient nonwords were about 69 percent.","**Regulars** were numerically worse than irregulars. Model regulars were about 80 percent, and model irregulars were about 85 percent."]},{"text":"Familiar words keep support that a new nonword lacks.","children":["So weak sound processing hurts nonwords most."]},{"text":"So a phonological impairment can look like a morphological deficit without removing any rule.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p020":[{"text":"Other patients show the reverse problem. They are **poor on irregulars**.","children":[]},{"text":"These groups are **Wernicke's** patients and **Alzheimer's** patients.","children":["Another group is **anomic** patients with injury to **inferior or middle temporal** brain regions.","Anomic patients have trouble retrieving words."]},{"text":"Irregulars are the lowest condition, at about 60 percent correct.","children":["Regulars are about 89 percent correct.","Nonwords are about 84 percent correct."]},{"text":"So these patients show the opposite side of the dissociation from Broca's patients.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p021":[{"text":"Damaging **semantics** in the model produced the irregular problem.","children":[]},{"text":"**Irregulars** were worse than regulars and nonwords.","children":["Model irregulars were about 62 percent correct.","Alzheimer's patients were about 60 percent on irregulars."]},{"text":"**Nonwords** were a bit worse than regulars.","children":["Model nonwords were about 77 percent, and model regulars were about 83 percent."]},{"text":"Irregulars rely on meaning more than other forms do.","children":["So losing semantic support hurts them most.","Regulars and nonwords still get support from typical sound patterns."]},{"text":"So the model reproduces the reverse profile without deleting any list of exceptions.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p022":[{"text":"Two studies tested whether the patient dissociation really separates rules from words.","children":[]},{"text":"**Bird, Patterson, Lambon-Ralph, McClelland and Seidenberg (2003)** studied **Broca's patients**.","children":["They compared regular and irregular past tenses.","They controlled the items adequately for **phonology**.","The difference between regulars and irregulars was **reduced**.","So part of the gap came from sound differences between the items."]},{"text":"**Patterson, Lambon-Ralph, Hodges and McClelland (2001)** studied **semantic dementia** patients.","children":["Semantic dementia is a loss of word meaning.","They compared each patient's irregular impairment with the severity of the semantic deficit.","Irregular impairment **correlated strongly** with semantic deficit severity.","So irregular trouble grows as meaning is lost."]},{"text":"So past-tense problems are tied to phonological and semantic abilities, not only to rules and words.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p023":[{"text":"This account parallels the **triangle model** of dyslexia.","children":[]},{"text":"**Phonological representations** support **generalization**.","children":["Generalization comes from **statistical learning** about patterns in the mapping from present to past.","Impaired phonological representations hurt these generalizations."]},{"text":"**Semantic representations** support **idiosyncratic** forms, which are forms particular to one word.","children":["Forms that deviate from the overwhelmingly regular \"add -ed\" pattern need support from semantics.","Some irregular forms can use **sub-patterns** shared with similar verbs.","Those irregulars are likely to be **spared**."]},{"text":"So regulars and irregulars draw on the same systems to different degrees.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p024":[{"text":"**Specific language impairment (SLI)** is **impaired language development** with \"normal\" performance or **nonverbal IQ**.","children":[]},{"text":"It affects **grammar** in two areas.","children":["**Morphology**, the structure of words, is affected.","**Syntax**, the structure of sentences, is affected."]},{"text":"It also affects abilities beyond grammar.","children":["These are **phonology**, **reading**, **working memory** and **speech perception**."]},{"text":"So SLI is broader than grammar alone. A theory that puts the problem only in grammar has to explain these other difficulties too.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p025":[{"text":"**Past-tense deficits** are a **sine qua non** of SLI. That means they are an essential feature of it.","children":[]},{"text":"Earlier claims said the SLI deficit was **specific to rules**.","children":["**Gopnik, van der Lely, Pinker, Ullman and Clahsen** made these claims.","On this view, SLI is a **deficit in learning rules**."]},{"text":"A rule deficit makes a clear prediction.","children":["People with SLI should have problems with **regulars** and **nonwords**.","They should not have problems with **irregulars**, because irregulars are stored words."]},{"text":"If this held, SLI could be evidence that language acquisition requires **domain-specific mechanisms**.","children":["Those are mechanisms used only for language."]},{"text":"So the account depends on whether irregulars are really spared in SLI.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p026":[{"text":"**van der Lely and Ullman (2001)** compared past-tense accuracy in an **SLI group** and **same-age controls**.","children":[]},{"text":"They tested regulars, irregulars and nonwords.","children":[]},{"text":"The **SLI group** was low on all three.","children":["Regulars were about 22 percent correct.","Irregulars were about 18 percent correct.","Nonwords were about 7 percent correct."]},{"text":"The **controls** were much higher on all three.","children":["Regulars were about 95 percent correct.","Irregulars were about 88 percent correct.","Nonwords were about 74 percent correct."]},{"text":"In SLI, irregulars were **not spared**.","children":["So the deficit is broader than a rule deficit predicts.","Nonwords were still the hardest."]},{"text":"So the data fit a broad impairment with an extra cost for nonwords.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p027":[{"text":"A model with weakened **phonology** learned the past tense poorly, and **nonwords** suffered most.","children":[]},{"text":"With a **severe phonological deficit**, scores were lowest.","children":["Regulars were about 39 percent correct.","Irregulars were about 17 percent, and nonwords were about 7 percent."]},{"text":"With a **weaker phonological deficit**, scores were higher but still low.","children":["Regulars were about 55 percent correct.","Irregulars were about 38 percent, and nonwords were about 15 percent."]},{"text":"The **intact** model did much better.","children":["At the end of training, regulars were about 100 percent correct. Irregulars were about 94 percent, and nonwords were about 80 percent.","At 700K iterations, regulars were about 90 percent. Irregulars and nonwords were both about 75 percent."]},{"text":"The impaired models have the same order as the SLI group.","children":["Regulars are best, irregulars are next, and nonwords are worst."]},{"text":"So weak phonology lowers all three forms without any missing rule.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p028":[{"text":"The model makes clear how **phonology** affects the development of **all** past-tense forms.","children":[]},{"text":"Every spoken past tense uses phonology.","children":["So weak phonology lowers regulars, irregulars and nonwords."]},{"text":"Phonology is **especially important** for **nonwords**.","children":["A nonword has no training history of its own.","So it depends heavily on generalizing a sound pattern.","Familiar words get extra support from item-specific learning."]},{"text":"So nonwords show the largest deficit, but every form still depends on phonology.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p029":[{"text":"This account is similar to the **triangle model** of reading.","children":[]},{"text":"There are no **special rules** for the past tense.","children":["There are only mappings between **phonology** and **semantics**."]},{"text":"There is no **separate lexicon** that stores the exceptions.","children":[]},{"text":"The ideas of **rule** and **exception** only approximately describe the system.","children":[]},{"text":"Two associations support the account.","children":["**Irregular** forms are associated with **semantic processing**.","**Wug test** performance is associated with **phonological processing**."]},{"text":"So past-tense behavior comes from learned mappings that lean on sound and meaning to different degrees.","children":[]}],"m3-morphology-p030":[],"m3-thought-p001":[],"m3-thought-p002":[{"text":"**Linguistic relativity** asks whether differences in language change how people organize or use concepts.","children":["The quotations from **Sapir (1929)** and **Whorf (1956)** suggest that **linguistic categories** can shape how experience is interpreted.","The strongest reading says language determines what a person can think.","A weaker reading says habitual language use biases attention, memory, or preferred ways of representing a situation."]},{"text":"These two readings need different evidence.","children":["The quotations are broad theoretical ideas, not experiments."]},{"text":"**Influencing thought** is different from **completely determining thought**.","children":["Keep that difference in mind when interpreting a difference between language groups.","So the real question is how strong the claim is, and what evidence can test it."]}],"m3-thought-p003":[{"text":"**Language is embedded in cultural practices and experience.**","children":["A difference between language communities may go along with factors other than linguistic structure.","Sapir and Whorf should not automatically be given the strongest reading of their quotations.","In the first half of the twentieth century, linguistics was part of **anthropology**.","So treating language as part of culture seemed more natural then than it does to linguists today."]},{"text":"**Testing linguistic relativity needs a narrower claim.**","children":["**Domains** (specific areas of experience) such as space and time let researchers test preferred ways of representing things.","A precise study must identify which **cognitive process** (a mental operation such as remembering) is affected.","It must also check which alternative explanations the comparison controls."]},{"text":"Historical framing explains why language and culture were discussed together.","children":["A study has to separate their contributions."]}],"m3-thought-p004":[{"text":"**A location description needs a frame of reference.**","children":["A **frame of reference** is the set of directions used to say where something is.","Saying where the ball is requires choosing what counts as left, front, or a stable direction.","The ball can be located relative to the viewer, relative to the chair's own orientation, or by a direction independent of both."]},{"text":"**Changing the reference changes the relation being described.**","children":["The object does not have to move for its verbal description to change.","Different descriptions can identify the same physical position.","The question is which reference determines the ball's location.","The chair and ball are the meaningful stimuli, not their appearance."]}],"m3-thought-p005":[{"text":"**An intrinsic frame uses an object's own orientation.**","children":["\"At the chair's front\" is an intrinsic description.","**A relative frame** uses the observer's viewpoint, so \"to the left of the chair from my viewpoint\" depends on the observer.","**An absolute frame** uses a stable direction, so \"north of the chair\" does not reverse when the observer turns around."]},{"text":"These are different ways to specify the same location.","children":["The object's orientation, the observer's body, and a stable outside direction are not interchangeable.","The ball and chair example keeps the scene the same and changes only the reference.","Rotating the observer changes relative left and right, but it does not change north."]},{"text":"This classification comes from **Levinson and colleagues (2002)**.","children":[]}],"m3-thought-p006":[{"text":"**Most languages describe nearby space egocentrically.**","children":["**Egocentric** descriptions use the speaker's own position and orientation.","Left, right, in front, and behind are familiar examples for nearby objects."]},{"text":"**Tzeltal commonly uses absolute directions instead.**","children":["Tzeltal is a Western Mayan language spoken in Chiapas.","It uses absolute directions in places where English speakers might use relative terms.","A small number of other languages also use absolute directions.","Those languages are mostly unrelated to one another and broadly distributed, such as in Bali and Queensland."]},{"text":"**The contrast raises a question about habits.**","children":["The question is whether habitual language use relates to nonverbal spatial coding.","The comparison concerns preferred spatial descriptions."]}],"m3-thought-p007":[{"text":"**Tzeltal's uphill and downhill terms work as an absolute directional system.**","children":["Things are uphill or downhill from one another, not to the left or right of the speaker.","\"Uphill\" and \"downhill\" are used as cardinal directions.","The system was historically based on landmarks."]},{"text":"**A landmark origin and an absolute use are different properties.**","children":["Each use does not simply point to the nearest visible hill.","The direction is kept as a stable reference instead of being recalculated from the speaker's current left and right."]},{"text":"**A stable direction keeps positions consistent when the speaker changes orientation.**","children":["The relevant feature is how the reference behaves across changes in location and orientation."]}],"m3-thought-p008":[{"text":"**New Yorkers use uptown and downtown as if they were cardinal directions.**","children":["These terms do not work outside Manhattan.","So they are literally a landmark-based system.","The subway map is also confusing about north and south.","Manhattan is angled with respect to magnetic north."]},{"text":"**A local landmark system is not an absolute frame that works everywhere.**","children":["Uptown and downtown organize movement within Manhattan.","A city's conventional map orientation can differ from magnetic north.","The Manhattan example is about reference systems, not about causation."]}],"m3-thought-p009":[{"text":"**Majid and colleagues (2004)** used a non-linguistic test for relative and absolute coding of space.","children":["In the **chips task**, the participant is rotated 180 degrees.","The participant sees a configuration, waits, and then chooses its match at a second table."]},{"text":"A **relative response** keeps the arrangement with respect to the person's viewpoint.","children":["An **absolute response** keeps its orientation in the environment.","After rotation, the two responses diverge and predict different choices."]},{"text":"**Dutch speakers predominantly gave relative responses.**","children":["**Tzeltal speakers gave more absolute responses.**","The north arrow stays fixed while the person changes orientation.","Red bars mark relative choices and blue bars mark absolute choices.","The association fits each group's preferred linguistic frame.","It does not isolate language from all other experience."]}],"m3-thought-p010":[{"text":"**Spatial inference can combine relations learned in different displays.**","children":["Participants learn the relation between two objects.","They rotate to another display and learn a second relation involving a shared object.","They then place one object relative to another using the combined information."]},{"text":"**Relative and absolute coding lead to different placements.**","children":["The person's orientation has changed between the displays.","The cube appears in both learning displays, linking the cone and cylinder relations.","The final placement separates a viewer-relative inference from one that keeps environmental direction."]},{"text":"**Dutch and Tzeltal response patterns differ again.**","children":["Each pattern follows the direction associated with that group's preferred spatial frame.","The task asks whether a frame is used when combining relations.","That goes beyond reproducing one remembered arrangement."]}],"m3-thought-p011":[{"text":"**Testing conditions may contribute to the apparent language difference.**","children":["**Li and Gleitman** noted that Tzeltal participants were tested outside, with visible, familiar landmarks.","Typical psychology lab studies are done in windowless rooms.","An environment with useful landmarks can encourage landmark-based coding.","A windowless room can make viewer-relative descriptions more convenient."]},{"text":"**A language comparison can be confounded with a setting comparison.**","children":["The environment must be considered before the whole difference is assigned to linguistic structure.","The critical contrast is access to spatial cues, not simply the label \"laboratory.\"","Windows, outdoor views, and familiar surroundings can alter which references are useful."]},{"text":"**Manipulating landmarks within English speakers tests this directly.**","children":["The test checks whether the behavior changes without changing native language."]}],"m3-thought-p012":[{"text":"**English speakers overwhelmingly used relative position when the blinds were down.**","children":["This resembles the Dutch speakers in earlier studies.","The preference was less pronounced with the blinds up and a view of the street.","It was also less pronounced outdoors."]},{"text":"**Changing the landmarks changes responses within one language group.**","children":["The language group stays the same while the spatial information changes.","So task context can influence the response.","This challenges an explanation in which language alone fixes the chosen frame."]},{"text":"**The change is a weakening, not a complete switch to absolute coding.**","children":["The observed change should be described without exaggerating it into an all-or-none reversal."]}],"m3-thought-p013":[],"m3-thought-p014":[{"text":"**If language groups are tested under different conditions, their response difference may partly reflect those conditions.**","children":["Visible landmarks and familiarity can covary with native language in a cross-community study.","A controlled comparison must separate the effect of the environment from any contribution of habitual linguistic coding."]},{"text":"One possibility is that the bias toward relative reasoning is an **artifact** (a result produced by how the study is run).","children":["So an uncontrolled comparison cannot separate language from testing conditions."]},{"text":"**A confound is a factor that changes with the variable of interest.**","children":["It offers another explanation for the result.","It is not just any feature present in the experiment."]}],"m3-thought-p015":[{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues were not impressed with the design.**","children":["They claimed that Li and Gleitman made the rotation too transparent.","Low memory load and short time or distance between displays make it easy to see what is being tested.","Li and Gleitman's participants were Ivy League students.","Ivy League students are good at figuring out what the experimenter wants."]},{"text":"**Participants who infer the purpose can change their responses.**","children":["This is a **demand-characteristic explanation** (the study's apparent purpose shapes participants' answers).","Interpreting the experiment can shift the response apart from normal coding preference.","So the response focuses on memory load and task transparency.","It does not deny that landmarks can ever matter."]}],"m3-thought-p016":[{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues (2002) re-ran the animals in a row experiment with Dutch college students.**","children":["They added a fourth animal and lengthened the delay to increase memory load.","They enlarged the distance between the stimulus and response tables to reduce the salience of the rotation."]},{"text":"**Under these conditions, the bias to use relative framing remained.**","children":["The extra item and longer delay make memory more demanding.","The larger distance makes the rotation less conspicuous.","So the environmental effect depends on how the task is arranged."]},{"text":"**Each procedural change has a purpose.**","children":["A relative preference can persist when the memory task is more demanding.","This narrows the conditions under which the landmark explanation accounts for behavior."]}],"m3-thought-p017":[{"text":"**Memory can make the relative preference hard to interpret.**","children":["The bias toward relative frames is robust to the environment when memory is a significant component of the task.","It is also robust when the experimenter's interest in rotation is not obvious.","Under memory demands, the relative preference could come from a verbal memory as well as a spatial one."]},{"text":"**Remembering the task can recruit verbal strategies as well as spatial ones.**","children":["So researchers need to ask how the sequence is stored during the delay.","They should not look only at which direction is chosen at the end."]},{"text":"**A task can be nonverbal in its response while still allowing verbal recoding internally.**","children":["The two conditions say when the effect is robust."]}],"m3-thought-p018":[{"text":"**Participants might remember the animals as a verbal list.**","children":["One example is the list \"lion, tiger, bear.\"","Participants could reconstruct that sequence from left to right.","That reconstruction can produce a response that looks relative."]},{"text":"**This strategy connects language to the task through working memory and ordering habits.**","children":["The same choice can arise from different internal codes."]},{"text":"**A list preserves order but not compass direction.**","children":["Translating that order into a left-to-right display can mimic a spatial frame preference.","A later page links reading direction to how sequences are arranged in space.","That link may help explain why left to right feels like a natural translation of a temporal sequence."]}],"m3-thought-p019":[{"text":"**Metaphorical language expresses one kind of experience using terms from another.**","children":["Calling an argument ineffective describes how well it works.","It is not a literal action with a blade.","Physical and spatial terms can help express abstract relations.","Time is especially useful for studying whether such habits go with nonlinguistic representations."]},{"text":"**An expression can be meaningful without being literal.**","children":["Whether spatial phrases about time go with spatial thinking about time is an empirical question."]},{"text":"**A lyric from Brown and Byrd (1970) is one example of metaphorical expression.**","children":["It raises the question of how much of language can safely be described as non-metaphorical."]}],"m3-thought-p020":[{"text":"**Boroditsky and colleagues (2010)** compared spatial metaphors for time in different languages.","children":["English and Mandarin both use horizontal spatial language for time.","Mandarin also frequently uses vertical terms.","An earlier event can be described with an \"up\" term, and a later event with a \"down\" term."]},{"text":"**The existence of these expressions is the starting observation.**","children":["A behavioral task is needed to investigate whether the vertical contrast extends beyond words."]},{"text":"**Habitual ways of talking about time suggest testable predictions about processing.**","children":["In these examples, \"up\" for an earlier month is a temporal use.","It is not a claim that the month occupies a physical location."]}],"m3-thought-p021":[{"text":"**Boroditsky (2001)** ran a priming experiment on spatial and temporal processing.","children":["Participants first process a horizontal or vertical spatial relation.","They then respond to questions about whether one event is earlier or later.","English speakers respond faster after horizontal primes than after vertical primes.","Mandarin speakers have the opposite pattern in the comparison tested."]},{"text":"**Short-term training changes the pattern.**","children":["Training English speakers on vertical metaphors for time moves their results toward a vertical prime advantage.","This suggests sensitivity to experience, not an unchangeable division between language groups."]},{"text":"**The prime changes which representation is active before the temporal judgment.**","children":["Response time differences reveal compatibility between the prime and the later task.","Lower reaction times mean faster processing.","So horizontal and vertical results should be compared within each group."]}],"m3-thought-p022":[{"text":"**Casasanto and Bottini (2005)** point to reading direction as an alternative explanation.","children":["Reading and writing habits can also organize sequences spatially.","Differences attributed to temporal metaphors may covary with whether text is read left to right, right to left, or vertically.","Casasanto and Bottini use differences in text orientation and arrangement preferences to support this idea."]},{"text":"**Language-group differences can bundle linguistic and literacy experiences together.**","children":["So reading direction needs to be measured or controlled.","The diagrams distinguish starting side from line direction.","The table lists several arrangement patterns within the sampled groups, so individual experience matters."]}],"m3-thought-p023":[{"text":"**Boroditsky and colleagues (2010)** excluded Chinese readers who reported reading top to bottom.","children":["This tests whether the vertical temporal effect can be explained entirely by that reported reading experience.","English speakers have an advantage for a left-is-earlier horizontal mapping.","Mandarin speakers also have an advantage for a top-is-earlier vertical mapping in the comparison tested."]},{"text":"**Retaining a vertical effect under this restriction weakens that reading explanation.**","children":["A targeted control tests one specific alternative."]},{"text":"**Lower reaction times indicate a mapping advantage.**","children":["White bars are canonical mappings, and black bars are noncanonical mappings.","For vertical trials, canonical means top is earlier."]}],"m3-thought-p024":[{"text":"**Casasanto and Bottini (2010)** had participants classify events as past or future with colored buttons.","children":["The color to category mapping was reversed across blocks.","So the past response was sometimes on the left and sometimes on the right."]},{"text":"**With standard text reading, responses are faster when past maps left and future maps right.**","children":["Because the response concerns time rather than an explicit spatial judgment, the effect reveals an implicit spatial association.","Color alone cannot explain the pattern, since the mappings are **counterbalanced** (the two mappings are used in turn)."]},{"text":"**The effect concerns response time across mappings.**","children":["It does not change which events count as past or future.","The left to right temporal mapping has the lower reaction times."]}],"m3-thought-p025":[{"text":"**Casasanto and Bottini (2010)** had participants read reversed text and then perform the past and future button task.","children":["By the second mirror-reading block, the compatibility effect reverses.","Responses are then faster when earlier maps right and later maps left."]},{"text":"**This result supports a contribution of reading direction experience.**","children":["The association between space and time can change over a short period.","A flexible temporal mapping is hard to explain as a fixed consequence of native language grammar alone.","Recent perceptual and reading experience contributes to performance."]},{"text":"**The first block keeps the usual advantage.**","children":["**The second block** favors the opposite mapping.","Compare the two colors within each block before comparing overall reaction times between blocks."]}],"m3-thought-p026":[{"text":"**Pullum (1989)** criticized an account of different word roots that became an exaggerated story about language and thought.","children":["His critique targets exaggerated claims about language and thought.","Claims about a language's vocabulary are not automatic evidence for cognitive limits.","Languages can express distinctions through separate roots, derived words, or phrases.","Counting one of these forms without considering the others can misrepresent expressive capacity."]},{"text":"**A reliable claim requires accurate linguistic description and an appropriate behavioral test.**","children":["Repeating a striking anecdote does not meet that standard."]},{"text":"**Languages can package the same distinctions in different ways.**","children":["The excerpt distinguishes roots from **derivationally related forms** (words built from another word) and phrases.","That distinction is why simple vocabulary counts can be misleading."]}],"m3-thought-p027":[],"m3-modalities-p001":[],"m3-modalities-p002":[{"text":"**Signed languages** and **Braille** are really different cases.","children":["A **signed language** is a **natural language** with its own linguistic structure.","**Braille** is a system that lets blind people read a spoken language they share with sighted people.","So Braille is a way of reading a language, not a separate language."]},{"text":"The two cases are studied together for one reason.","children":["Both reveal the **plasticity** of sensory systems. Plasticity is the ability of the brain to change its organization with experience.","Both reveal how the brain gets organized differently when language input comes from a different **modality**. A modality is the sense the input arrives through, such as vision or touch."]},{"text":"So the shared theme is modality. The linguistic status of the two cases still differs, and a comparison should keep that difference.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p003":[{"text":"**Emmorey and colleagues (2004)** describe several obvious ways that **sign languages** differ from spoken languages.","children":["The first difference is **modality**. Sign languages are seen rather than heard.","The second difference is the **articulators**, the body parts that produce the language. In sign, the hands, face and upper body produce it.","The third difference is the **use of space**. Where a sign is made can carry linguistic relations."]},{"text":"The fourth difference is **iconicity**. A sign is iconic when its form resembles its meaning.","children":["Iconicity is **easy to exaggerate**. It seems strongest when you look at single signs with their glosses.","A **gloss** is the translation word used to name a sign. Once you know the gloss, a resemblance looks obvious.","A learner still has to learn the forms and structure of the language."]},{"text":"So sign languages differ from speech in surface form. They are still **structured languages**, not collections of transparent gestures.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p004":[{"text":"**Fluent signing** coordinates several articulators at the same time.","children":["The **hands**, the **face** and the **upper body** move together.","**Eye contact** is also part of the interaction.","So the linguistic signal is spread across all of these, not carried by one hand at a time."]},{"text":"Fluent signing is also **fluid** and **fast**.","children":["Signs run into each other in a continuous stream.","Can you segment the words? For a learner, the **word boundaries** are often not obvious.","Fluent speech works the same way, because words in a spoken stream also run together."]},{"text":"That is why the thing to study is coordinated language production, not an isolated hand shape.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p005":[{"text":"Like speech, **sign** can be described with **features**. This is the **phonology of sign language**.","children":["**Emmorey and colleagues (2003)** list three features that can differ **contrastively** between signs.","**Hand configuration** is the shape the hand takes.","**Location** is where the gesture is made.","**Movement** is how the hand moves."]},{"text":"A feature is **contrastive** when changing it changes which sign it is.","children":["For **\"please\"** and **\"sorry\"**, the two signs are closely related forms with different meanings. They differ in **hand configuration**.","For **\"onion\"** and **\"apple\"**, the two signs are also closely related forms with different meanings.","In speech, changing one feature of a sound can change the word in the same way."]},{"text":"So phonology is about how contrastive form is organized. It is not only about sound.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p006":[{"text":"The research question is whether signers show **categorical perception** for these sign features.","children":["**Categorical perception** means perceiving a smooth series of small changes as a few separate categories.","It shows up as a sharp identification boundary and better discrimination near that boundary."]},{"text":"**Emmorey and colleagues (2003)** built two **continua** of hand configurations. Each continuum steps through intermediate hand shapes.","children":["The **phonemic** continuum runs from the sign **PLEASE** to the sign **SORRY**. Its two ends are different signs.","The **allophonic** continuum runs from **open-N** to **closed-N** in the sign **SAY-NO-TO**. Its two ends are the same sign."]},{"text":"**Deaf signers** did two tasks with these images.","children":["In **identification**, they decided which sign each image was.","In **discrimination**, they decided whether two images were different."]},{"text":"Both continua change visually, but only the phonemic one crosses a contrast. So comparing them separates a physical change from a category change. The answer is yes, but only for the phonemic continuum.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p007":[{"text":"The **deaf signers** of **Emmorey and colleagues (2003)** showed the familiar pattern of categorical perception. They showed it for the phonemic continuum only.","children":["For the **phonemic** continuum, identification changed sharply between **stimuli 4 and 6**.","That sharp change is the **categorical boundary**.","**Discrimination** peaked at that same boundary. So steps that crossed the boundary were easier to tell apart."]},{"text":"For the **allophonic** continuum, the pattern was different.","children":["The identification function was **much shallower**. So many more stimuli were ambiguous.","There was **no peak** in discrimination."]},{"text":"Equal physical steps were treated differently depending on the linguistic contrast. So categorical perception follows the **phonemic contrast** in sign, not any visible change in the hand.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p008":[{"text":"**Sign language** uses **space** to mark relationships between words in a sentence.","children":["A **classifier sign** stands for an object. Where it is made gives the position of that object.","In a very transparent case, the **relative position of two objects** is given by where the classifier sign is made."]},{"text":"Sentences of any complexity work this way too.","children":["A signer places things at locations in the signing space.","Later signs relate back to those locations.","So the person watching must **keep track of where signs were made**."]},{"text":"That is why **spatial processing** is part of understanding sign grammar. It is not just background vision.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p009":[{"text":"**Campbell, MacSweeney and Waters (2008)** compared brain activity for sign comprehension and spoken language.","children":["They looked at **deaf signers** and **hearing signers** watching sign.","They also looked at **hearing participants** watching **audio-visual speech**."]},{"text":"Some activity was **similar** across sign and speech.","children":["Both were **left lateralized**, meaning activity was stronger in the left hemisphere.","Both used the **classical language areas**. These are **Broca's area** (inferior frontal) and **Wernicke's area** (posterior superior temporal)."]},{"text":"Some activity was **different** for sign.","children":["Sign used more **visual areas**.","Sign also used **superior parietal** areas, which are classic spatial areas.","These fit the visual and spatial demands of a signed signal."]},{"text":"So sign and speech share a core language network. Sign adds areas tied to its modality.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p010":[{"text":"**Bavelier and colleagues (2001)** asked whether **deafness**, **experience with sign**, or both affect **spatial attention**.","children":["They tested **hearing controls** who did not sign.","They tested **deaf signers** who were native signers.","They tested **hearing signers** who were also native signers."]},{"text":"Participants watched a field of dots.","children":["They monitored for brief changes in **luminance**, which is brightness.","Or they monitored for brief changes in **velocity**, which is speed of motion.","Attention was directed either to the **center** of the screen or to the **periphery**."]},{"text":"**Deaf participants** showed an **advantage** when attention was directed to the **periphery**.","children":["Hearing signers did not share it, so the advantage goes with deafness, not with signing experience.","Deafness and signing come bundled in deaf signers. The hearing signers pull them apart."]},{"text":"So **deafness**, not signing experience, is tied to better peripheral attention.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p011":[{"text":"Deafness and signing experience have **two different effects**.","children":["Each effect goes with a different group."]},{"text":"**Learning sign language** changes the **laterality** of motion perception areas.","children":["Laterality is which hemisphere a function relies on more.","In hearing nonsigners, motion areas lean to the **right**.","In **deaf signers** and **hearing signers**, they lean to the **left**.","Perhaps motion perception needs to \"talk to\" left-lateralized language networks in people who learn sign."]},{"text":"**Enhanced peripheral visual attention** is found only in **deaf** individuals.","children":["Hearing signers do not show it, even though they sign.","Perhaps hearing people rely more on **auditory cues** for spatial attention at the far periphery."]},{"text":"So **signing** changes lateralization, while **deafness** enhances peripheral attention.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p012":[{"text":"Researchers wanted to separate two things that sign comprehension mixes together.","children":["One is the **visual, spatial and motor processing** of hand movements.","The other is **language processing**."]},{"text":"They compared sign comprehension directly with **tic-tac**.","children":["**Tic-tac** is a sophisticated and outmoded **gestural system**.","It was used in the UK to communicate odds at horse races."]},{"text":"Both use organized, sequenced hand movements that carry meaning.","children":["Only sign is a natural language.","So the hand-movement demands are shared by both.","Any extra activity for sign then reflects **language**."]},{"text":"That is why tic-tac is a better control than no movement at all.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p013":[{"text":"**Campbell, MacSweeney and Waters (2008)** compared brain activity for sign language and for tic-tac.","children":["Sign language produced **stronger activity around Wernicke's area**.","Wernicke's area is in the **posterior superior temporal** region."]},{"text":"Hand movement alone cannot explain this.","children":["Both sign and tic-tac involve organized hand movements.","Only sign is a language.","So the extra activity near Wernicke's area goes with **language**."]},{"text":"The **frontal activation** in this comparison sits **superior to Broca's area**.","children":["So it should not be called a Broca's area effect."]},{"text":"That is why sign is processed as language, not just as hands moving through space.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p014":[{"text":"The evidence about signed language comes down to four points.","children":[]},{"text":"Sign language has **phonological structure**.","children":["**Native signers** show **categorical perception** for some contrastive features.","This holds for phonemic hand configuration but not for an allophonic change."]},{"text":"Learning a signed language leads to some **brain reorganization**.","children":["The clearest case is the **lateralization of motion processing**.","It shifts from right to left in signers."]},{"text":"Attention to the **visual periphery** matters for both hearing and deaf signers.","children":["Still, only **deaf signers** show **enhanced** peripheral attention."]},{"text":"Signed sentences activate **posterior STG**, which is **Wernicke's area**.","children":["STG is the superior temporal gyrus.","The comparison was with **tic-tac**, which also uses organized, sequenced hand movements."]},{"text":"So sign is a real language, and deafness and signing each shape the brain in their own way.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p015":[{"text":"**Emmorey (2002)** studied ASL descriptions of where a table sits in a room.","children":["The table is in the same place in both descriptions.","Only the **perspective** of the description changes."]},{"text":"The description can come from the **speaker's perspective**.","children":["The signer signs **I-ENTER**, **TABLE**, **THERE**.","The layout is set up as the signer would see it."]},{"text":"The description can come from the **addressee's perspective**.","children":["The signer signs **YOU-ENTER**, **TABLE**, **THERE**.","The layout is set up as the viewer would see it."]},{"text":"Signers **comprehend better** when the room is described from the **speaker's perspective**.","children":["This holds even though the viewer must **mentally transform** the layout."]},{"text":"So the easier description is not the one that removes every spatial transformation.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p016":[{"text":"**Braille** is **tactile reading**, which means reading through touch.","children":["Braille reading is in many ways **equivalent to reading print**.","It is still reading the same spoken language."]},{"text":"Braille differs from print in two ways.","children":["The **symbols** are **optimized for tactile discrimination**. So they are easy to tell apart by touch.","Reading Braille by sight is really hard, because the symbols were not built for the eye.","The **fingers take in letters one at a time**. So letters arrive in sequence, not as a whole word at once."]},{"text":"So the task stays reading while the sensory input changes. That is why Braille is useful for studying how reading networks work with touch.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p017":[{"text":"**Reading Braille activates visual cortex** in blind individuals.","children":["The input is touch, not sight.","Visual cortex still responds to it."]},{"text":"The comparison is between **early blind** and **late blind** participants.","children":["For **early blind** participants, visual activity to tactile stimuli is **more extensive**.","For **late blind** participants, it is less extensive.","So the **earlier sight is lost**, the more visual cortex responds to touch."]},{"text":"The **visual word form area** follows the same pattern.","children":["It is a region linked to processing written words.","It is **strongly activated** in early blind participants.","It is activated **more** in early blind than in late blind participants."]},{"text":"So a region's usual sense does not fully decide what it can process. The timing of sensory loss shapes how it gets used.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p018":[{"text":"An earlier question was whether **competition** could keep hearing people from developing enhanced peripheral visual attention.","children":["Competition means that a strong input can crowd out a weaker one.","**Merabet and colleagues (2008)** tested competition in a different way."]},{"text":"They asked **sighted** people to **wear blindfolds for five days**.","children":["During those days, they **trained** them to read **Braille**.","A **control group** was also trained but did not wear blindfolds."]},{"text":"The **blindfolded group learned more quickly** than the control group.","children":["The blindfold removed visual input.","So touch faced less competition from vision."]},{"text":"That is why removing a competing input can speed up learning, even in adults over a short time.","children":[]}],"m3-modalities-p019":[{"text":"**Merabet and colleagues (2008)** also scanned the blindfolded participants while they read Braille.","children":["On **Day 1**, primary visual cortex showed little response to Braille.","Over the course of the experiment, **primary visual cortex** started to respond.","By **Day 5**, the response was largest."]},{"text":"On **Day 6**, the **blindfold was removed**.","children":["The effect was **completely reversed**.","Visual cortex no longer responded to Braille in the same way."]},{"text":"So the recruitment of visual cortex for touch was **rapid** and **reversible**.","children":["It appeared within days.","It disappeared once vision came back.","That is why it looks like a change in competition, not a new pathway built from scratch."]}],"m3-modalities-p020":[{"text":"**Transcranial magnetic stimulation (TMS)** was used to **disrupt** activity in visual cortex. TMS uses a magnetic pulse to interfere with a brain area.","children":["For the **blindfolded group**, TMS **disrupted Braille reading** on Day 5. Their **error rate** went up.","For the **non-blindfolded group**, TMS did not disrupt reading in the same way.","On **Day 6**, after the blindfold came off, the effect was gone."]},{"text":"This is evidence that blindfolded participants were somehow **using visual cortex for tactile processing**. It is still marked with a \"maybe.\"","children":["Activation alone only means a region responds.","Disruption changing behavior means the region **contributes** to the task."]},{"text":"Visual cortex may get **tactile input all the time**, even in sighted people.","children":["That input is **too weak to compete** with the stronger, more consistent visual input.","So even short-term removal of vision can **\"unmask\"** tactile responses in visual cortex."]}],"m3-modalities-p021":[{"text":"**Language circuitry is resilient** despite differences in modality.","children":["For **sign language**, the **classical language areas** are activated. This happens even though the input differs dramatically in surface form.","For **Braille**, reading seems to engage networks that are important for **processing text**."]},{"text":"**Cross-modal plasticity** is the reuse of brain areas by input from a different sense.","children":["It suggests that brain organization is **shaped over development by competition**.","When **auditory input is unavailable**, putative auditory areas are used to process sign.","In the same case, **visual attention is enhanced at the periphery**."]},{"text":"Competition also mattered for second-language learning.","children":["Competing cognitive and perceptual processes that shape language processing were important in the **Competition Model** of second-language learning."]},{"text":"So competition among inputs and processes shapes language processing across modalities and across languages.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p001":[],"m3-review-p002":[{"text":"**Orthographic depth** describes how consistently a script's spelling matches its pronunciation. A **shallow** script has regular letter-sound matches. A **deep** script has many irregular ones.","children":["**English** is a relatively deep script because of its irregularities.","**Finnish** and **Korean** are very shallow scripts.","**Pinyin** for Mandarin and **French** are relatively regular."]},{"text":"A **\"mispronouncing contest\"** made the point with words like COLONEL and PARADIGM.","children":["People willfully mispronounced these words by following their letters.","The contest made clear that **phonemes are not letters**. It also made clear that **letters are not phonemes**.","In COLONEL and PARADIGM, the letters do not reliably give the sounds."]},{"text":"So a reader of a deep script cannot always decode a word from its letters alone. That is why English readers must learn many exceptions through experience.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p003":[{"text":"Finnish is a very **transparent** writing system. The **Orthographic Depth Hypothesis** calls such a system **shallow**.","children":["Each letter corresponds more or less unambiguously to one **phoneme**.","**Doubled letters** mark length. A doubled consonant is a **geminated** consonant, which is held longer. A doubled vowel is a long vowel."]},{"text":"A beginning Finnish lesson lets a listener follow the sounds along with the written text.","children":["After about a minute, most listeners get the idea of how letters map onto sounds."]},{"text":"Finnish children learn to **decode** very quickly. Decoding means producing a pronunciation from a written form.","children":["One learned letter-sound match works across many different words."]},{"text":"So a shallow script makes decoding fast to learn and easy to generalize.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p004":[{"text":"English is an **outlier** among languages that use the **Roman alphabet**. Its spelling is far less regular than most of them.","children":[]},{"text":"The comparison uses **pseudowords**. A pseudoword is a made-up word that follows the spelling and sound patterns of a language.","children":["A child has never seen a pseudoword, so it cannot be read from memory.","The child must **decode** it from learned letter-sound patterns."]},{"text":"In **Grade 1**, children learning a wide variety of other languages read pseudowords well. They produce plausible pronunciations at a **much higher rate** than English learners.","children":["For **children learning English**, the spelling patterns are less predictable.","For **children learning more regular scripts**, the patterns are consistent, so decoding develops faster."]},{"text":"So one result of English's depth is that **children learn to decode more slowly in English**.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p005":[{"text":"Korean writing is **even more regular than Finnish**. Its symbols for **similar phonemes** look similar to one another.","children":["So the shape of a letter reflects how its sound is made.","Letters are grouped into **syllable blocks** with initial, medial and sometimes final positions."]},{"text":"**Velar place of articulation** has its own written feature. Velar sounds are made with the back of the tongue at the soft palate.","children":["The velar consonants share the basic angular shape **ㄱ**.","Doubling the shape or adding a stroke tells the related velar consonants **g**, **kk** and **k** apart."]},{"text":"So in Korean, a family of related sounds is written with a family of related shapes.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p006":[{"text":"The **frequency by regularity interaction** is a landmark finding in psycholinguistics. It is measured with **naming latency**, the time needed to start saying a written word.","children":["The **frequency effect** is slower naming of low-frequency words than high-frequency words.","A **regular word** follows the usual spelling-sound pattern. An **exception word** does not."]},{"text":"The frequency effect is **much larger for exception words** than for regular words.","children":["For **high-frequency words**, exception and regular words are both named in about 540 ms.","For **low-frequency words**, exception words rise to about 580 ms. Regular words rise only to about 555 ms."]},{"text":"An **interaction** means the effect of one factor depends on the other factor.","children":["Here the cost of being an exception depends on how frequent the word is.","The finding has impacted other areas, most immediately **inflectional morphology**."]},{"text":"So experience matters most when a word's pronunciation conflicts with its spelling pattern.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p007":[{"text":"One interpretation of the interaction uses **two separate processes** in word recognition.","children":["The **sublexical process** is decoding. It computes a pronunciation from spelling units.","The **lexical process** is knowledge of a particular word. It supplies that word's learned pronunciation."]},{"text":"The two processes matter differently case by case.","children":["For **high-frequency (HF) words**, the lexical process is fast and efficient. So the sublexical process has little influence.","For **low-frequency (LF) words**, the lexical process is slower. So the sublexical output shows up.","For **nonwords**, there is no stored word. So reading depends on the sublexical process."]},{"text":"A low-frequency exception word gets a regularized pronunciation from decoding. That output conflicts with the real pronunciation and slows naming.","children":[]},{"text":"That is why regularity matters most for low-frequency words in this account.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p008":[{"text":"**Seidenberg and McClelland (1989)** built a **PDP model** of visual word recognition. A PDP model is a network that learns connections between spelling and sound.","children":[]},{"text":"It makes different assumptions from dual-route models.","children":["**Rules** are just generalizations from lots of words. They are not a separate process.","The **lexicon** is also distributed. Some whole-word information is stored in the **orthography to phonology (O to P) mappings**. Some is stored in other parts of the system."]},{"text":"It also uses a different research strategy.","children":["Dual-route models reverse-engineer the reading system from adult and **neuropsychological** data.","The PDP approach explains reading with **domain-general learning mechanisms**. These mechanisms are not specific to reading or language."]},{"text":"So in a PDP model, word-specific knowledge and general patterns live in one learned system.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p009":[{"text":"The **Seidenberg and McClelland (1989)** model captured the **frequency by regularity interaction**. It did not assume that frequency and regularity come from different processes.","children":[]},{"text":"The model learns from the statistics of many words.","children":["**Regular words** are consistent with the statistical patterns of many other words. So they get support from those words.","**Exception words** are inconsistent with these statistics. So they get no such support.","**Frequency** affects how well each word is learned. A frequent word is trained many times."]},{"text":"The two factors combine case by case.","children":["A **high-frequency exception** is learned so well that its inconsistency matters little.","A **low-frequency exception** has weak learning and no support from other words. So it is the slowest.","A **low-frequency regular word** still gets support from the shared patterns."]},{"text":"So one learning mechanism can produce the whole interaction.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p010":[{"text":"A PDP model reads **words and nonwords in the same route**. A **nonword** is a made-up letter string with no stored entry.","children":["In principle, nonwords are named with the **same set of weights** used for words. **Weights** are the learned connection strengths in the network.","No separate rule system for nonwords is needed."]},{"text":"**Generalization** to nonwords uses the same **statistical patterns** that produce regularity effects.","children":["A regular word is easy because it shares patterns with many words.","A new nonword is pronounced by applying those same shared patterns."]},{"text":"So, in principle, one learned mapping handles familiar words and new items alike.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p011":[{"text":"In practice, the **Seidenberg and McClelland** model did not read nonwords well. Its **nonword accuracy** was not very good.","children":[]},{"text":"The failure is a classic case of **ambiguity in model evaluation**. A poor result has two possible causes.","children":["One cause could be that the **theoretical claim** is wrong. The claim is that nonwords and exceptions can be read by the same associative mechanism.","The other cause could be something specific to **how the model was made**. That is a problem of implementation, not of theory."]},{"text":"The way to decide is to repair the model.","children":["If a better implementation reads nonwords well, the theory survives.","The later improvement came from better **phonological representations**."]},{"text":"So a failed simulation leaves both causes open until the model is repaired.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p012":[{"text":"Nonword reading is tied to **phonological representations**, the way a system codes speech sounds.","children":["Many studies find a relationship between **decoding** and **phonological processing**. Decoding here means nonword reading."]},{"text":"**Seidenberg and McClelland (1989)** used odd phonological representations called **Wickelfeatures**.","children":["Wickelfeatures captured sound similarity poorly.","So the model's nonword reading suffered."]},{"text":"**Plaut and colleagues (1996)** improved the phonological representations. Their model produced **better performance on nonwords**.","children":["The improvement came without adding a separate rule system."]},{"text":"So at least part of the earlier failure came from the implementation, not the theory.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p013":[{"text":"**Generalization depends on similarity**. A network handles a new item through its similarity to items it has learned. This principle is inherited from **behaviorism**.","children":[]},{"text":"**Poor phonological representations**, like Wickelfeatures, fail to capture the **similarity space** of words in the language. The similarity space is how words relate to each other in sound.","children":["When similarity is coded badly, learning cannot transfer well to new forms."]},{"text":"The damage differs for words and nonwords.","children":["For **familiar words**, the network has **word-specific information** from training on that word. That information does not depend entirely on generalization. So it is **more robust** to poor representations.","For **nonwords**, there is no word-specific information. Reading depends fully on generalization. So nonwords suffer most."]},{"text":"That is why poor phonology hurts nonword reading more than word reading.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p014":[{"text":"**Harm and Seidenberg (1999)** studied the link between phonology and nonword generalization more systematically. They manipulated the amount of **noise** in the model's phonological representations.","children":["More noise makes the sound representations less reliable.","The rest of the learning model stays the same."]},{"text":"The noisy models replicated key findings on **developmental dyslexia**, a reading impairment that appears during development.","children":["The degree of **phonological deficit** went with the degree of **phonological dyslexia**. Phonological dyslexia is poor nonword decoding.","**Word recognition** was relatively spared."]},{"text":"So one weakened phonological system can produce a selective-looking reading deficit.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p015":[{"text":"**Pinker and Ullman (2002)** proposed a **dual-route model** for the past tense. The input is a **word stem** such as walk or hold plus a **grammatical feature** such as past tense.","children":["The **lexicon** route works by lookup and association. It supplies irregulars such as held.","The **grammar** route combines a stem with a suffix. It produces regulars such as walk plus -ed.","A **blocking** connection lets a retrieved irregular stop the regular form."]},{"text":"The two routes belong to different memory systems.","children":["The lexicon is part of **declarative memory**, tied to words and facts. Its main substrate is temporo-parietal cortex.","The grammar is part of the **procedural system**, tied to rules and skills. Its main substrate is frontal cortex and basal ganglia."]},{"text":"The model is **similar** to the reading dual-route model. Both separate **word-specific knowledge** from a **productive operation**.","children":["It is also **different**. Reading converts spelling into sound. The past-tense model changes a stem according to a grammatical feature."]},{"text":"So similar architectures can solve different problems.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p016":[{"text":"The past-tense dual-route model looks like the reading model, but it has **different origins**.","children":[]},{"text":"The reading dual-route model was built from **neuropsychological double dissociations**. These are patients whose impairments pattern in opposite ways.","children":[]},{"text":"**Pinker and colleagues** were motivated by other concerns.","children":["**Generative linguistics** of the time was mostly about categorical, symbolic, rule-based grammars.","These rules often have **exceptions**.","So their model keeps the rule system apart from exceptional lexical information."]},{"text":"So the diagrams look similar, but the theories grew from different evidence.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p017":[{"text":"Linguists often left **exceptions** out of the grammar. They did not want to make the grammar inelegant by adding machinery for them.","children":[]},{"text":"The **dual-process model** proposes a way to handle the exceptions. The grammar stays elegant, symbolic and categorical.","children":[]},{"text":"The second process has all the properties the rule route lacks.","children":["It is **word-specific**, which means lexical.","It is **sensitive to frequency** of occurrence.","It is **sensitive to phonological similarity**. Keep-kept, weep-wept and leap-leapt form a family of similar irregulars."]},{"text":"So exceptions go to an associative lexicon, and the rule stays simple.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p018":[{"text":"**Rumelhart and McClelland (1987)** offered an alternative approach. In their approach, the past tense is **all associative learning**.","children":[]},{"text":"One trainable network learns the past tense across all verbs.","children":["It encodes the sound of a verb root as **Wickelfeatures**.","A **pattern associator** with modifiable connections maps the root pattern onto a past-tense pattern.","The output pattern is then decoded into a past-tense form."]},{"text":"The network has no separate -ed rule.","children":["**Regular forms** are a pattern learned from many examples.","**Irregular forms** are learned in the same system."]},{"text":"So one mechanism is proposed for both regulars and exceptions.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p019":[{"text":"Rumelhart and McClelland's model was one of the first learning models of a language process. So its focus was on capturing **developmental phenomena**.","children":[]},{"text":"Its main target was **U-shaped learning**.","children":["First, children learn some frequent irregular verbs quite early, such as run-ran.","Then, spontaneously, children begin producing **regularizations** such as runned. A regularization applies the regular past-tense pattern to an irregular verb.","Eventually, children produce the correct forms again."]},{"text":"The learning is called **U-shaped** because children often get worse before they get better. Performance goes down and then goes back up.","children":[]},{"text":"So a theory must explain the whole path of learning, including the temporary errors.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p020":[{"text":"U-shaped learning matters to the **words and rules** approach because the approach explains it with distinct stages and processes.","children":[]},{"text":"Each stage gets its own process.","children":["At first, early correct forms are just **imitations**. They do not depend on the same mechanisms as adult processing.","When the **rule** is first learned, it is applied to all cases. The rule is the best hypothesis for all items generally. So the child says runned.","Over time, **exceptions** are learned associatively, one by one. They become part of lexical knowledge.","The **blocking process** also develops. A retrieved irregular then blocks the regular form."]},{"text":"So in this account, the dip in performance marks the arrival of the rule.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p021":[{"text":"Rumelhart and McClelland's model also produces **U-shaped learning** with only one network.","children":[]},{"text":"The **training set** is based on data about the input to children. That input changes over time.","children":["Early on, **irregular verbs** predominate. The highest-frequency verbs tend to have irregular past tenses, such as GIVE, TAKE, HAVE and GO.","So the model first learns these irregular mappings."]},{"text":"Later the vocabulary expands to include more **regular forms**. The model then reorganizes to capture those regularities.","children":["This reorganization **interferes** with some early-learned forms. So the model starts getting them wrong.","With further training, the irregular responses recover."]},{"text":"So a change in experience can produce the U shape without a separate rule component.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p022":[{"text":"The model was trained on a **small number of verbs**. So the authors could run a **\"wug\" test with real words**. A wug test asks a learner to produce the past tense of a word it has not learned.","children":["A real English verb is still new to the model if the model was never trained on it."]},{"text":"The test used **unfamiliar low-frequency regular verbs**.","children":["Many responses were correct regular forms, such as guarded, mapped and typed.","Some responses were odd, such as shipped for shape and toureder for tour."]},{"text":"The model generated **92 percent** of **Wickelfeatures** correctly for regular verbs. It generated 84 percent for irregular verbs and 91 percent overall.","children":["A high share of correct features does not mean the same share of whole words came out right."]},{"text":"So generalization is measured relative to what the learner was trained on.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p023":[{"text":"The model also produced some **unusual outputs**. Just four responses added or changed consonants.","children":["It gave maded as a past tense of mate.","It gave squawked for squat.","It gave membled for mail.","It gave toureder for tour."]},{"text":"The model's authors said **humans are unlikely to make these errors**, especially the last two.","children":["These responses were, for the most part, **near threshold**.","The authors suggested adding an **auto-associative network** among the output units. It could clean up the output pattern and avoid bizarre responses.","They had not yet implemented this suggestion."]},{"text":"So a good overall learning curve is not enough if a model makes errors that people would not make.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p024":[{"text":"**Joanisse and Seidenberg (1999)** built a newer model of the past tense. It links **speech input**, **semantics** and **speech output** in one system.","children":[]},{"text":"The model rests on two theoretical premises.","children":["**Rule-like generalization** is supported by **phonological representations**. So regular and new forms lean mainly on sound.","**Irregular forms** require greater support from **semantics**, the meaning of the particular word."]},{"text":"So the difference between regulars and irregulars comes from different reliance on phonology and semantics. There is no separate rule route.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p025":[{"text":"The **Joanisse and Seidenberg (1999)** model targets a **dissociation** in neuropsychological data. Some patients do worse on **irregular** past tenses. Others do worse on **nonword** past tenses.","children":[]},{"text":"The model explains each side with a different kind of support.","children":["A **nonword** like wug has no meaning. So its past tense depends on **phonology**, and damage to phonology should hurt nonwords most.","An **irregular** verb does not follow the regular pattern. So it depends more on **semantics**, and damage to semantics should hurt irregulars most."]},{"text":"So one architecture can produce opposite patterns of impairment.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p026":[{"text":"**Broca's aphasia** gives one side of the dissociation.","children":["It comes from injury to **left anterior** brain regions.","Patients have **poor speech production**."]},{"text":"These patients have problems with **regular morphology**, especially with **nonwords**.","children":["They struggle with forms like wug to wugged and blick to blicked.","Nonwords were the lowest, at about 69 percent correct.","Regular verbs were about 82 percent correct. Irregular verbs were about 85 percent correct."]},{"text":"A nonword has no stored past tense, so it must be built by generalization. So this pattern points to a **phonological** contribution to the deficit.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p027":[{"text":"The other side of the dissociation is **problems with irregulars**.","children":["It appears in **Wernicke's** and **Alzheimer's** patients.","It also appears in **anomic** patients with injury to inferior or middle temporal brain regions."]},{"text":"These patients are **poor on irregular** past tenses.","children":["Irregular verbs were about 60 percent correct.","Regular verbs were about 89 percent correct. Nonwords were about 84 percent correct."]},{"text":"So these patients show the **reverse** of the Broca's pattern. That fits damage to the **semantic** support that irregulars need.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p028":[{"text":"The classical view of aphasia is an **attractive dichotomy** between two syndromes.","children":[]},{"text":"**Wernicke's aphasia** produces \"word salad\" or \"Jabberwocky,\" often with **neologisms**, which are made-up words.","children":["Syntax stays roughly intact.","One patient described his poor vision as \"My wires don't hire right.\"","Another patient described a fork as \"a need for a schedule.\""]},{"text":"**Broca's aphasia** produces **telegraphic** speech.","children":["Patients have difficulty with **word order** and **grammatical particles**.","Important **content words** are relatively spared.","One patient described his hospital visit in fragments such as \"Two doctors ... and ah ... teeth.\""]},{"text":"So Wernicke's speech is fluent but hard to interpret. Broca's speech is effortful and made mostly of content words.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p029":[{"text":"The dichotomy treats **Wernicke's aphasia** as problems with **activation** in the lexicon, or perhaps inhibition. It treats **Broca's aphasia** as problems with **grammar**.","children":[]},{"text":"This view is fairly distant from how **Broca** described his original cases.","children":["His patient **Leborgne**, called \"Tan,\" could only produce one jargon syllable.","He seemed otherwise to have his senses. He also seemed to understand what was being said."]},{"text":"This point is confusing. Broca's actual patients would **not necessarily be classified as Broca's aphasics**.","children":["They were arguably unable to produce speech at all.","The cause was apparently **motor control** issues.","Today they would be classified as having **apraxia of speech**, a problem planning or coordinating speech movements."]},{"text":"So the modern label names a grammar problem, while Broca's own patients had a motor control problem.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p030":[{"text":"**Wernicke's aphasia** is a problem with activation in the **lexicon**, and **Broca's aphasia** is a problem with **grammar**.","children":["This dichotomy is attractive partly because it matches a sharp distinction in late 20th century **formal linguistics**. That distinction separates the **grammar** from the **lexicon**.","The grammar combines forms into sentences.","The lexicon holds words and their properties."]},{"text":"The distinction starts with **Chomsky's** formulation of the **computational problem of language learning**.","children":["People recognize a new sentence because the grammar they have internalized generates it.","The new sentence does not have to match any familiar sentence."]},{"text":"**Chomsky (1955)** also argued for the **independence of syntax and semantics**.","children":["Neither \"colorless green ideas sleep furiously\" nor \"furiously sleep ideas green colorless\" had occurred in an English speaker's experience.","Yet the first is grammatical and the second is not."]},{"text":"So a grammar and lexicon split seemed to fit the two aphasia types neatly.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p031":[{"text":"The speech production model also separates a **functional level** from a **positional level**.","children":["It begins at the **message level**, which arouses concepts and event structure.","The **functional level** arouses word meanings and semantic roles.","The **positional level** gets word forms into a grammatically appropriate order.","**Phonological encoding** and the **speech gesture level** come after these."]},{"text":"The two levels differ in kind.","children":["**Lemmas** at the functional level have an **arbitrary** relationship to their referents. A lemma is the word representation chosen during word selection.","**Syntactic trees** and **affixes** at the positional level are products of **abstract syntactic processes**."]},{"text":"Words like HOUSEBOAT and DOGHOUSE seem to raise an objection, because their meaning is clearly not arbitrary.","children":["Speech errors answer this objection. Errors such as \"easy enoughly\" place **derivational morphology** at the positional level."]},{"text":"So the functional and positional levels line up with the lexicon and grammar split.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p032":[{"text":"Most languages talk about nearby space **egocentrically**. Egocentric terms use the speaker's own body as the reference point.","children":["Examples are left and right, in front of and behind."]},{"text":"**Tzeltal** does not do this. It is a Western Mayan language spoken in Chiapas.","children":["Tzeltal uses **absolute directions** instead. Absolute directions stay fixed when a person turns.","A small number of other languages do the same. They are mostly unrelated and broadly distributed, for example in Bali and Queensland."]},{"text":"For English speakers, a request to \"raise your east hand\" feels strange.","children":[]},{"text":"So languages differ in which spatial frame they use for nearby things.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p033":[{"text":"Tzeltal has a historically **landmark-based** system.","children":["Things are either **uphill** or **downhill** from one another.","They are not to the left or right of the speaker or the listener."]},{"text":"\"Uphill\" and \"downhill\" are used as **cardinal directions**. So Tzeltal has an **absolute** system.","children":["The terms come from the slope of the landscape around Tenejapa.","In use, they act as fixed directions. They do not simply point to the nearest hill.","**Levinson and colleagues (2002)** described this system."]},{"text":"So a direction keeps the same meaning as the speaker turns around.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p034":[{"text":"**Majid and colleagues (2004)** used a **non-linguistic test** of **relative** versus **absolute** coding of space. It is called the **chips task**.","children":[]},{"text":"A participant sees a card at Table 1 with a large dot and a small dot.","children":["After a 30-second delay, the participant is **rotated 180 degrees** and led to Table 2.","The participant then picks the matching card from a set of four."]},{"text":"The rotation makes the two codings predict different cards.","children":["A **relative** response keeps the arrangement the same with respect to the person's body.","An **absolute** response keeps the arrangement the same with respect to the environment, such as north."]},{"text":"**Dutch** speakers gave overwhelmingly **relative** responses. **Tzeltal** speakers gave mostly **absolute** responses.","children":["So nonverbal spatial memory matched each language's preferred frame."]}],"m3-review-p035":[{"text":"**Majid and colleagues (2004)** also tested **spatial inference**. The task combines relations learned at two tables.","children":[]},{"text":"At Table 1, participants see a cube and a cone.","children":["They are rotated 180 degrees to Table 2 and see the cube with a cylinder.","They are rotated back to Table 1, where the cone stands alone.","They must place the cylinder next to the cone, consistent with what they saw."]},{"text":"The two codings give different answers.","children":["With **relative** coding, the cube is right of the cone and the cylinder is right of the cube. So the cylinder goes right of the cone.","With **absolute** coding, the cube is south of the cone and the cylinder is further south. So the cylinder goes south of the cone."]},{"text":"**Dutch** participants gave **relative** responses. **Tzeltal** participants gave **absolute** responses. So each group used its preferred frame even when reasoning about space.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p036":[{"text":"**Li and Gleitman** questioned the Tzeltal results. They tried **inducing landmark-based similarity in English speakers**.","children":[]},{"text":"They looked at the conditions of testing.","children":["**Tzeltal speakers** were tested outside, with visible and familiar **landmarks**.","Typical psychology lab studies are done in **windowless rooms**."]},{"text":"Useful landmarks can encourage absolute coding. A windowless room offers none, so relative coding is more convenient.","children":["So the language difference could partly be a **testing-environment** difference."]},{"text":"That is why they tested English speakers under different viewing conditions.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p037":[{"text":"**Li and Gleitman** tested English speakers on the rotation task under three conditions.","children":[]},{"text":"With the **blinds down**, English speakers overwhelmingly used **relative position**. They behaved like the Dutch speakers in prior studies.","children":["With the **blinds up** and a view of the street, their relative preference was **less pronounced**.","**Outdoors**, their relative preference was also less pronounced."]},{"text":"The language group stayed the same. Only the available landmarks changed.","children":["A weaker relative preference is not a full switch to absolute coding."]},{"text":"So the testing environment can change which spatial frame people use.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p038":[{"text":"**Levinson and colleagues** were not impressed by Li and Gleitman's results. They raised two objections.","children":[]},{"text":"First, Li and Gleitman made it too **transparent** that they were testing rotation.","children":["They **reduced memory load**.","They shortened the **time and distance** between the stimulus and the test.","An easy, obvious task invites a deliberate strategy instead of habitual coding."]},{"text":"Second, the participants were **Ivy League students**.","children":["Such students excel at figuring out what the experimenter wants and then making it happen."]},{"text":"So the landmark effect might reflect guessing the purpose, not normal spatial memory.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p039":[{"text":"The research question asks whether **categorical perception** occurs for sign language **hand configuration**. In categorical perception, a gradual change is perceived as a sudden switch between categories.","children":[]},{"text":"Two continua of signs were created.","children":["The **phonemic** continuum changes hand configuration from the sign PLEASE to the sign SORRY. So it crosses a contrast between two signs.","The **allophonic** continuum changes from open-N to closed-N in the sign SAY-NO-TO. So it changes the hand without changing the sign."]},{"text":"Observers make **identification** and **discrimination** judgments along each continuum.","children":["Categorical perception predicts a sharp identification boundary.","It also predicts the best discrimination at that boundary."]},{"text":"So the test separates a physical change from a linguistically meaningful one.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p040":[{"text":"**Deaf signers** show **categorical perception** for the **phonemic** hand-configuration contrast. Their curves have the familiar categorical shape.","children":[]},{"text":"For the **phonemic** continuum, identification has a fairly sharp **categorical boundary** between stimuli 4 and 6.","children":["This boundary lines up with the **peak** in the **discrimination function**.","So pairs that cross the boundary are the easiest to tell apart."]},{"text":"For the **allophonic** continuum, the identification function is **much shallower**.","children":["There are many more ambiguous stimuli.","There is **no peak** in the discrimination function."]},{"text":"So categorical perception follows the linguistic contrast, not just any visible change in the hand.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p041":[{"text":"Brain activity during sign language comprehension has both **similarities** to and **differences** from spoken language. **Campbell, MacSweeney and Waters (2008)** compared deaf signers, hearing signers and hearing people perceiving audio-visual speech.","children":[]},{"text":"Sign and speech are similar in two ways.","children":["Both are **left lateralized**.","Both use the **classical language areas**. These are Broca's area in inferior frontal cortex and Wernicke's area in posterior superior temporal cortex."]},{"text":"Sign differs in two ways.","children":["It recruits more **visual areas** and fewer **auditory areas**.","It also recruits **superior parietal** cortex, a classic spatial area."]},{"text":"So sign uses the same core language network, plus areas suited to a visual and spatial signal.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p042":[{"text":"Sign language has **phonological structure**. Native signers show **categorical perception** for some contrastive features.","children":[]},{"text":"Learning a signed language leads to some **brain reorganization**.","children":["The clearest case is the **lateralization of motion processing**."]},{"text":"Attention to the **visual periphery** is critical for both hearing and deaf signers.","children":["Only **deaf signers** show enhanced processing of visual attention in the periphery.","So this enhancement comes with deafness, not with signing alone."]},{"text":"Signed sentences activate **posterior STG**, which is Wernicke's area.","children":["The comparison was non-language stimuli that also involve organized, sequenced hand movements.","So this activation reflects language, not just hand movement."]},{"text":"So sign is a full language with its own phonology, and learning it also reorganizes the brain.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p043":[{"text":"**Johnson and Newport (1989)** described two versions of the **critical period hypothesis**. Both say that early in life humans have a superior capacity for acquiring languages.","children":[]},{"text":"The **exercise hypothesis** ties the outcome to use.","children":["If the capacity is not exercised early, it disappears or declines with maturation.","If the capacity is exercised, later language learning abilities remain intact throughout life."]},{"text":"The **maturational state hypothesis** ties the outcome to maturation alone.","children":["The capacity disappears or declines with maturation, whatever the early experience."]},{"text":"Johnson and Newport see the **maturational state hypothesis** as more consistent with their data.","children":["Their late second-language learners had already exercised the capacity with a first language.","Yet later starters still learned the second language less well."]},{"text":"This changes how the **L1 critical period** data can be read.","children":["Late first-language learners never exercised the capacity early. So both versions predict their poor outcome.","Only second-language data can tell the two versions apart. Early first-language experience still matters."]}],"m3-review-p044":[{"text":"**Mayberry, Lock and Kazmi (2002)** asked how early language experience affects later language learning.","children":[]},{"text":"Many **deaf children** get no sign language input, but they have an otherwise typical upbringing.","children":["Children aged 9 to 15 have difficulty learning **American Sign Language (ASL)** when it is their first language.","A comparison group went deaf after first learning a **spoken language**. These children do much better.","The differences **persist into adulthood**."]},{"text":"Deaf adults were tested on ASL sentence recall.","children":["Those with no early language scored about 36 percent. Those with an early spoken language scored about 78 percent."]},{"text":"Adults were also tested on English grammar judgments.","children":["Deaf adults with no early language scored about 52 percent, near the 50 percent chance level.","Adults with an early signed or early spoken language scored about 80 percent."]},{"text":"So what matters is early access to some language, whether spoken or signed.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p045":[{"text":"A **semantic blocking effect** is slower naming of objects when same-category objects are named together. One possible **confound** is **visual object recognition**.","children":["Objects in the same category may look alike.","So the effect might come from seeing the objects, not from naming them."]},{"text":"There were two responses to this confound.","children":["First, the stimuli were **normed**. On average, same-category objects were not more visually similar than objects from different categories.","Second, a **control experiment** blocked objects by **visual similarity** instead. It found different results."]},{"text":"In the control, the visual blocking effect appeared in the **first block of trials**.","children":["The semantic effect appears only after the first cycle."]},{"text":"So the semantic blocking effects are not likely due to visual similarity.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p046":[{"text":"**Hermens and colleagues** had people see **multiple objects simultaneously**. They found **no blocking effect**.","children":["This raised a worry that the effect depends on seeing objects one at a time, in serial."]},{"text":"**Belke and colleagues** addressed this worry.","children":["Participants viewed displays of **four items simultaneously**.","They produced the four names **in serial**, one after another."]},{"text":"This design separates how objects are shown from how names are produced.","children":["All objects are visible at once.","Naming still requires selecting one word after another."]},{"text":"So if blocking appears here, it does not depend on viewing objects in serial.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p047":[{"text":"**Belke and colleagues** measured **eye movements** while participants named the four objects.","children":[]},{"text":"Each object gives two timing measures.","children":["**Gaze duration** is how long the eyes stay on an object.","**Eye-speech lag** is the time between the eyes leaving an object and the start of saying its name."]},{"text":"The eyes run ahead of speech.","children":["A speaker often looks at the next object before articulating the current name.","So looking at an object and saying its name are separate events in time."]},{"text":"So each measure can track a different stage of preparing a name.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p048":[{"text":"Each timing measure maps onto a stage of speech production. The comparison is **homogeneous** blocks, with same-category objects, against **heterogeneous** blocks, with mixed objects.","children":[]},{"text":"**Overall speech duration** decreases over the experiment. It shows **no blocking effect**.","children":["Speech duration reflects the **gesture level**, which is articulation."]},{"text":"**Gaze duration** shows a **blocking effect**, but only after the **first cycle**.","children":["Gaze duration reflects **lemma selection**."]},{"text":"**Eye-speech lag** shows **no effect**.","children":["Numerically the effect seems to go the opposite way, but this is **not significant**.","Lag reflects **phonological encoding**."]},{"text":"So the blocking effect arises at **lemma selection**, not at articulation or phonological encoding.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p049":[{"text":"**McMurray, Tanenhaus, Aslin and Spivey (2003)** asked how **phoneme identification** feeds **word recognition**.","children":["**Thresholded** processing, like a lumber mill, passes on only a finished category decision.","**Cascaded** processing, like a food web, lets graded information flow on before the decision is final."]},{"text":"They tested a **/b/ to /p/ continuum** in a **visual world** display with eye tracking.","children":["The continuum varies **voice onset time (VOT)**. VOT is the delay between releasing the consonant and the start of voicing.","Each display held a target such as a pear and a competitor such as a bear. It also held two fillers, a lamp and a ship."]},{"text":"So looks to the competitor can reveal whether fine phonetic detail reaches word recognition.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p050":[{"text":"**McMurray and colleagues (2003)** first measured participants' final choices. Participants used the mouse to select which of the pictures matched what they heard.","children":["For example, they chose between a **bear** and a **pear**."]},{"text":"The mouse choices formed a typical **step-like identification function**.","children":["Short VOTs gave /b/ responses, so participants chose the bear.","Long VOTs gave /p/ responses, so participants chose the pear.","The **category boundary** was at about 17.5 ms of VOT."]},{"text":"A categorical final choice can still come from graded processing before it. So eye movements are needed to track the processing before the choice.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p051":[{"text":"Eye movements reveal that **ambiguity is gradient**. There are **more looks to the competitor** close to the category boundary.","children":[]},{"text":"The effect holds within one chosen category.","children":["When participants chose the **bear**, VOTs closer to the boundary drew more looks to the pear.","When participants chose the **pear**, VOTs closer to the boundary drew more looks to the bear.","VOT mainly changes how **long** the competitor stays active."]},{"text":"So fine phonetic detail inside a category still affects **lexical activation**. That favors **cascaded** processing over thresholded processing.","children":[]}],"m3-review-p052":[{"text":"The gradient effect is clearest when competitor looks are compared step by step across **VOT**. Looks are split into an **early window** of 300 to 1100 ms and a **late window** of 1100 to 1900 ms.","children":[]},{"text":"For **bear responses**, looks to the pear rise as VOT moves from 0 ms up toward the boundary.","children":["For **pear responses**, looks to the bear rise as VOT moves from 40 ms down toward the boundary.","The pattern appears in both the early and the late window."]},{"text":"So the closer a sound is to the boundary, the more the competitor word stays active. That is why the results support **cascaded** processing.","children":[]}]}};
